export const ARTICLES = [
  { tool: "compose", slug: "overview", title: "What Compose is", section: "start", order: 1,
    summary: "Music notation by tapping — nothing is guessed, and every bar adds up.",
    keywords: ["compose","notation","start","intro","what","piano","grand staff"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Two promises","id":"two-promises"},{"level":2,"text":"What you can write today","id":"what-you-can-write-today"},{"level":2,"text":"What is not here yet","id":"what-is-not-here-yet"},{"level":2,"text":"Where to go next","id":"where-to-go-next"}],
    html: "<p>Compose is music notation you write by <strong>tapping</strong>. You arm a value on the Notes rail — a quarter, an eighth, a dotted half — and tap the staff. The note lands on the nearest line or space at the nearest slot of that value, and you hear it as it lands.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/editor.png\" alt=\"The Compose editor: the header, the rails, and the score below them.\" width=\"2388\" height=\"1668\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Compose editor: the header, the rails, and the score below them.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"two-promises\">Two promises</h2>\n<p><strong>Nothing is guessed.</strong> There is no handwriting recognition anywhere in Compose, and there never will be. A tap places exactly the value you armed at exactly the place you tapped. Your intent lives in the palette; the staff only confirms it. That is the whole reason this exists: a notation app that guesses wrong is slower than paper.</p>\n<p><strong>A bar always adds up.</strong> Every measure sums to its time signature, always. The gaps are rests, rests are real things, and placing a note eats the rest under it. Nothing can be half-written. If an edit would break that sum, it is <strong>refused</strong> and nothing changes — see <a href=\"#/compose/help/nudges\">when an edit is refused</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-can-write-today\">What you can write today</h2>\n<p>A piano grand staff — treble and bass — with up to <strong>four voices</strong> on each staff and notes that cross between them. Values from the double whole to the 64th, with dots, ties and tuplets. Chords, accidentals, articulations and ornaments. Key, time and clef changes anywhere in the piece. Dynamics, hairpins, text and pedal on the beat and the half-beat. Repeats, endings, jumps, rehearsal marks and tempo changes. Grace notes, tremolo and trills. Pickup bars.</p>\n<p>It prints as a <strong>vector PDF</strong> — real outlines, not a picture of a page — which you can lay out bar by bar first, drop straight into your <a href=\"#/scores\">Scores</a> library, or send to a student. It reads and writes <strong>MusicXML</strong>, so a piece travels to Sibelius, MuseScore, Finale or Dorico and back.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-not-here-yet\">What is not here yet</h2>\n<p>Lyrics, chord symbols, more than two staves, parts, and MIDI export. The model is shaped like MusicXML, so each of those is an addition rather than a rewrite.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-to-go-next\">Where to go next</h2>\n<ul><li>Never opened it before — <a href=\"#/compose/help/first-composition\">your first composition</a>.</li><li>Want the lay of the land — <a href=\"#/compose/help/editor\">the editor screen</a> and <a href=\"#/compose/help/modes\">Place, Select and Pan</a>.</li><li>Looking for a button — <a href=\"#/compose/help/rails\">how the rails work</a>.</li><li>Ready to print — <a href=\"#/compose/help/export-pdf\">export a PDF</a> and <a href=\"#/compose/help/layout\">the Layout editor</a>.</li></ul>",
    text: "Compose is music notation you write by tapping. You arm a value on the Notes rail — a quarter, an eighth, a dotted half — and tap the staff. The note lands on the nearest line or space at the nearest slot of that value, and you hear it as it lands. !The Compose editor: the header, the rails, and the score below them. Two promises Nothing is guessed. There is no handwriting recognition anywhere in Compose, and there never will be. A tap places exactly the value you armed at exactly the place you tapped. Your intent lives in the palette; the staff only confirms it. That is the whole reason this exists: a notation app that guesses wrong is slower than paper. A bar always adds up. Every measure sums to its time signature, always. The gaps are rests, rests are real things, and placing a note eats the rest under it. Nothing can be half-written. If an edit would break that sum, it is refused and nothing changes — see when an edit is refused. What you can write today A piano grand staff — treble and bass — with up to four voices on each staff and notes that cross between them. Values from the double whole to the 64th, with dots, ties and tuplets. Chords, accidentals, articulations and ornaments. Key, time and clef changes anywhere in the piece. Dynamics, hairpins, text and pedal on the beat and the half-beat. Repeats, endings, jumps, rehearsal marks and tempo changes. Grace notes, tremolo and trills. Pickup bars. It prints as a vector PDF — real outlines, not a picture of a page — which you can lay out bar by bar first, drop straight into your Scores library, or send to a student. It reads and writes MusicXML, so a piece travels to Sibelius, MuseScore, Finale or Dorico and back. What is not here yet Lyrics, chord symbols, more than two staves, parts, and MIDI export. The model is shaped like MusicXML, so each of those is an addition rather than a rewrite. Where to go next - Never opened it before — your first composition. - Want the lay of the land — the editor screen and Place, Select and Pan. - Looking for a button — how the rails work. - Ready to print — export a PDF and the Layout editor." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "first-composition", title: "Your first composition", section: "start", order: 2,
    summary: "From a blank grand staff to a PDF, in about ten minutes.",
    keywords: ["new","start","tutorial","walkthrough","first","blank","create"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"1 · Make one","id":"1-make-one"},{"level":2,"text":"2 · Place four notes","id":"2-place-four-notes"},{"level":2,"text":"3 · Write a chord, and a bass line","id":"3-write-a-chord-and-a-bass-line"},{"level":2,"text":"4 · Hear it","id":"4-hear-it"},{"level":2,"text":"5 · Keep going","id":"5-keep-going"},{"level":2,"text":"6 · Print it","id":"6-print-it"},{"level":2,"text":"Then","id":"then"}],
    html: "<h2 id=\"1-make-one\">1 · Make one</h2>\n<p>Open Compose and tap <strong>new</strong>. Give the piece a title and, if you like, a composer and tags — the same details a score in your library carries. Tap <strong>start composing</strong> and a blank piano score opens: treble and bass, C major, 4/4, eight empty bars, and the <strong>quarter already armed</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/compositions-list.png\" alt=\"The compositions list. Tap new to start; hold a row for its details.\" width=\"1112\" height=\"422\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The compositions list. Tap new to start; hold a row for its details.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"2-place-four-notes\">2 · Place four notes</h2>\n<p>Tap the treble staff four times, roughly on the first four beats. Each tap puts a quarter note on the nearest line or space at the nearest quarter slot, and sounds it. Before the tap, a faint <strong>ghost</strong> shows exactly what will land and where.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-ghost-note\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-ghost-note\">The ghost shows the note the tap will place, before it places it.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"40\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"47\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"47\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"54\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"54\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"61\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"68\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-ghost\" x=\"86\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-ghost hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"90.4\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"90.4\" y2=\"38.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"86\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the ghost</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"112,54 146,54\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"146,54 141,56.4 141,51.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"180\" y=\"61\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"184.4\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"184.4\" y2=\"38.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"180\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the note you placed</text><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"120\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">what the tap will do, before it does it</text></svg><figcaption>The ghost shows the note the tap will place, before it places it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Wrong pitch? Put the pen down <strong>on the notehead</strong> and drag up or down — it moves by staff step, sounding each one, and the release commits it. Wrong value? Tap the note, then tap a different duration on the Notes rail: the note becomes that value, and that value is armed for the next tap too.</p>\n<p>Wrong note altogether? Tap it and press <strong>Delete</strong> — a rest takes its place, because a bar always adds up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-write-a-chord-and-a-bass-line\">3 · Write a chord, and a bass line</h2>\n<p>Tap a <strong>different pitch at the same slot</strong> as a note you already placed and the pitch joins the chord. Duration, dots and articulations belong to the chord as a whole; accidentals and ties belong to each pitch.</p>\n<p>Now tap the lower staff. Nothing about the staves is modal — the pen decides which one it is writing on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-hear-it\">4 · Hear it</h2>\n<p>Press <strong>play</strong> on the Transport rail, or the space bar. Playback starts from the playhead at the piece's tempo, follows repeats and tempo marks, and the slider seeks. <strong>Home</strong> stops.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-keep-going\">5 · Keep going</h2>\n<p>A new empty bar appears the moment the last one gets its first note, so you never run out of paper. Everything is <strong>saved as you write</strong> — there is no save button, and there never will be. With an account, the piece syncs to your other devices.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-print-it\">6 · Print it</h2>\n<p><strong>File ▾ → Export PDF</strong> opens the export sheet: page size, staff size, margins, a header, and a live preview of the real page. <strong>Save to device</strong> or <strong>Share…</strong> gets you the file; <strong>Save to Scores as PDF</strong> drops it into your <a href=\"#/scores\">Scores</a> library, where a later send replaces it in place.</p>\n<p>If you want bars 1–4 on the first row and bar 10 given extra room because it is dense, the <strong>Layout</strong> row in that sheet opens <a href=\"#/compose/help/layout\">the Layout editor</a> and you arrange it on the paper itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"then\">Then</h2>\n<p>Turn on <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">Gesture mode</a> — it is off until you ask for it — and the common moves stop needing a trip to Select.</p>",
    text: "1 · Make one Open Compose and tap new. Give the piece a title and, if you like, a composer and tags — the same details a score in your library carries. Tap start composing and a blank piano score opens: treble and bass, C major, 4/4, eight empty bars, and the quarter already armed. !The compositions list. Tap new to start; hold a row for its details. 2 · Place four notes Tap the treble staff four times, roughly on the first four beats. Each tap puts a quarter note on the nearest line or space at the nearest quarter slot, and sounds it. Before the tap, a faint ghost shows exactly what will land and where. !The ghost shows the note the tap will place, before it places it. Wrong pitch? Put the pen down on the notehead and drag up or down — it moves by staff step, sounding each one, and the release commits it. Wrong value? Tap the note, then tap a different duration on the Notes rail: the note becomes that value, and that value is armed for the next tap too. Wrong note altogether? Tap it and press Delete — a rest takes its place, because a bar always adds up. 3 · Write a chord, and a bass line Tap a different pitch at the same slot as a note you already placed and the pitch joins the chord. Duration, dots and articulations belong to the chord as a whole; accidentals and ties belong to each pitch. Now tap the lower staff. Nothing about the staves is modal — the pen decides which one it is writing on. 4 · Hear it Press play on the Transport rail, or the space bar. Playback starts from the playhead at the piece's tempo, follows repeats and tempo marks, and the slider seeks. Home stops. 5 · Keep going A new empty bar appears the moment the last one gets its first note, so you never run out of paper. Everything is saved as you write — there is no save button, and there never will be. With an account, the piece syncs to your other devices. 6 · Print it File ▾ → Export PDF opens the export sheet: page size, staff size, margins, a header, and a live preview of the real page. Save to device or Share… gets you the file; Save to Scores as PDF drops it into your Scores library, where a later send replaces it in place. If you want bars 1–4 on the first row and bar 10 given extra room because it is dense, the Layout row in that sheet opens the Layout editor and you arrange it on the paper itself. Then Turn on Gesture mode — it is off until you ask for it — and the common moves stop needing a trip to Select." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "editor", title: "The editor screen", section: "editor", order: 1,
    summary: "The header, the rails and the score — and what each part of it is for.",
    keywords: ["editor","screen","layout","anatomy","header","score","zoom","systems","bars","save"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"The header","id":"the-header"},{"level":2,"text":"The rails","id":"the-rails"},{"level":2,"text":"The score","id":"the-score"},{"level":2,"text":"The ghost","id":"the-ghost"},{"level":2,"text":"Bars","id":"bars"},{"level":2,"text":"Sound","id":"sound"},{"level":2,"text":"Saving","id":"saving"}],
    html: "<p>The editor is three things stacked: a <strong>header</strong>, however many <strong>rails</strong> you have asked for, and the <strong>score</strong> filling everything below them.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-anatomy-editor\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-anatomy-editor\">The editor: header, rails, and the score below.</title><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel\" x=\"12\" y=\"6\" width=\"216\" height=\"108\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-band\" x=\"18\" y=\"12\" width=\"204\" height=\"13\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"21.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">File ▾ · title · Options ▾</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-band\" x=\"18\" y=\"28\" width=\"204\" height=\"12\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"36.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Controls · Transport</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-band\" x=\"18\" y=\"42\" width=\"204\" height=\"12\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"50.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Notes · the rails you show</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-band\" x=\"18\" y=\"58\" width=\"204\" height=\"50\" rx=\"3\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"66\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"73\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"73\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"80\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"87\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"87\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"30\" y1=\"94\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"94\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"60\" y=\"87\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"64.4\" y1=\"87\" x2=\"64.4\" y2=\"64.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"104\" y=\"76.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"99.6\" y1=\"76.5\" x2=\"99.6\" y2=\"98.9\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"148\" y=\"83.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"152.4\" y1=\"83.5\" x2=\"152.4\" y2=\"61.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">the score — it never moves while you write</text></svg><figcaption>The editor: header, rails, and the score below.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"the-header\">The header</h2>\n<p><code>‹</code> goes back to your compositions. The <strong>title</strong> in the middle is a button — tap it for the details sheet, where the title, composer and tags live. <strong>File ▾</strong> holds the exports. <strong>Options ▾</strong> is the control panel: what draws, gestures, favorites, which rails show, and help. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">the Options panel</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rails\">The rails</h2>\n<p>Controls and Transport and Notes are shown by default; the other five are a tick away in Options. Each rail is <strong>one lane, always</strong> — when it does not fit it scrolls sideways under a finger, and it never wraps to a second line, so the score never loses room without you asking. <a href=\"#/compose/help/rails\">How the rails work</a> explains the grammar they all share.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-score\">The score</h2>\n<p>Systems wrap to the width of the screen and stack down the page, exactly as the PDF will page them. <strong>What you edit is what you print</strong> — there is no separate print view to be surprised by, only the <a href=\"#/compose/help/layout\">Layout editor</a> if you want to arrange the paper deliberately.</p>\n<p><code>−</code> and <code>+</code> on the Controls rail zoom by re-drawing at a new staff size, not by scaling a picture, so the glyphs stay crisp at every step. Your zoom is remembered per device.</p>\n<p><strong>The score holds still.</strong> In Place and Select nothing pans, nothing zooms, and a palm resting on the glass does nothing at all. Panning is <a href=\"#/compose/help/modes\">its own mode</a>, and a mouse wheel always scrolls because a desk has no palm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-ghost\">The ghost</h2>\n<p>On a pen or a mouse, a faint copy of the note the next tap will place follows the tip — the right head, stem, dots and flag, at the pitch and slot it will land on. On a finger there is no hover, so the ghost appears when you touch down and the note lands where the ghost is when you lift.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bars\">Bars</h2>\n<p>A piece starts with eight empty bars, and a <strong>new one is appended the moment the last bar gets its first note</strong>. Trailing empty bars are trimmed when you export and when you close, so a stray empty measure never reaches a PDF. To put a bar in the middle, or take one out, use insert and delete on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">the Form rail</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sound\">Sound</h2>\n<p>A placed note sounds, a chord sounds together, and every step of a re-pitch drag sounds as it passes. Selecting is silent. The volume follows the app's own.</p>\n<h2 id=\"saving\">Saving</h2>\n<p>Every committed edit saves itself, at once, on this device. Reopening restores the piece and your zoom. With an account it all syncs. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/saving\">saving and your library</a>.</p>",
    text: "The editor is three things stacked: a header, however many rails you have asked for, and the score filling everything below them. !The editor: header, rails, and the score below. The header ‹ goes back to your compositions. The title in the middle is a button — tap it for the details sheet, where the title, composer and tags live. File ▾ holds the exports. Options ▾ is the control panel: what draws, gestures, favorites, which rails show, and help. See the Options panel. The rails Controls and Transport and Notes are shown by default; the other five are a tick away in Options. Each rail is one lane, always — when it does not fit it scrolls sideways under a finger, and it never wraps to a second line, so the score never loses room without you asking. How the rails work explains the grammar they all share. The score Systems wrap to the width of the screen and stack down the page, exactly as the PDF will page them. What you edit is what you print — there is no separate print view to be surprised by, only the Layout editor if you want to arrange the paper deliberately. − and + on the Controls rail zoom by re-drawing at a new staff size, not by scaling a picture, so the glyphs stay crisp at every step. Your zoom is remembered per device. The score holds still. In Place and Select nothing pans, nothing zooms, and a palm resting on the glass does nothing at all. Panning is its own mode, and a mouse wheel always scrolls because a desk has no palm. The ghost On a pen or a mouse, a faint copy of the note the next tap will place follows the tip — the right head, stem, dots and flag, at the pitch and slot it will land on. On a finger there is no hover, so the ghost appears when you touch down and the note lands where the ghost is when you lift. Bars A piece starts with eight empty bars, and a new one is appended the moment the last bar gets its first note. Trailing empty bars are trimmed when you export and when you close, so a stray empty measure never reaches a PDF. To put a bar in the middle, or take one out, use insert and delete on the Form rail. Sound A placed note sounds, a chord sounds together, and every step of a re-pitch drag sounds as it passes. Selecting is silent. The volume follows the app's own. Saving Every committed edit saves itself, at once, on this device. Reopening restores the piece and your zoom. With an account it all syncs. See saving and your library." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "modes", title: "Place, Select and Pan", section: "editor", order: 2,
    summary: "Three modes, and what a pointer does in each of them.",
    keywords: ["mode","place","select","pan","modes","escape","esc","arm","armed"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Place","id":"place"},{"level":2,"text":"Select","id":"select"},{"level":2,"text":"Pan","id":"pan"},{"level":2,"text":"Getting out","id":"getting-out"}],
    html: "<p>Compose has exactly three modes. The switch on the Controls rail shows the two you choose between; <strong>Place</strong> is where you are when neither is lit.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-modes-triangle\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-modes-triangle\">The three modes and the ways between them.</title><ellipse class=\"hp-fig-panel\" cx=\"64\" cy=\"36\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"17\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"64\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Place</text><ellipse class=\"hp-fig-panel\" cx=\"176\" cy=\"36\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"17\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"176\" y=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Select</text><ellipse class=\"hp-fig-panel\" cx=\"120\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"34\" ry=\"17\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"120\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Pan</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"98,30 142,30\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"142,30 137,32.4 137,27.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\">tap the armed value</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"142,42 98,42\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"98,42 103,39.6 103,44.4\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"56\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Esc</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"152,52 134,78\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"134,78 134.9,72.6 138.8,75.3\"/><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"106,78 88,52\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"88,52 92.8,54.7 88.9,57.4\"/></svg><figcaption>The three modes and the ways between them.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"place\">Place</h2>\n<p>The default, and where you spend your time. A value is armed on the Notes rail, and a tap on the staff places it.</p>\n<ul><li>A tap on <strong>empty staff or a rest</strong> places the armed value at the nearest slot and pitch.</li><li>A tap on a <strong>notehead</strong> grabs it at once — it selects, and a vertical drag re-pitches it by staff step, sounding each one. The release commits. The mode and the armed value are untouched, so the next tap elsewhere still places.</li><li>A tap at an <strong>existing note's slot but a different pitch</strong> joins the chord.</li><li>A <strong>long press</strong> on empty staff draws a marquee, so you can select without leaving Place.</li><li><strong>Hold two seconds</strong> in one spot and the <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">Favorites palette</a> comes to your hand.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"select\">Select</h2>\n<p>Reached by the switch, by <code>v</code>, by <code>Esc</code>, or by tapping the armed duration on the palette a second time — which clears the arming, and clearing the arming <em>is</em> Select. There is no armed value, so nothing places.</p>\n<ul><li>A tap <strong>selects</strong> a note, a rest, a whole chord by its stem, or a mark; a second tap toggles it.</li><li>A pointer down on <strong>empty staff that moves</strong> draws a freehand lasso; lifting selects everything inside it.</li><li>The same grab-and-drag on a notehead works here too.</li><li>A tap on empty staff that never moves <strong>clears</strong> the selection.</li></ul>\n<p>With a selection, every palette and rail button acts on it instead of arming. That is the one rule behind every button in Compose — see <a href=\"#/compose/help/selection\">selecting, moving, deleting</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pan\">Pan</h2>\n<p>The hand. One finger or the pen pans; two fingers pinch to zoom. <strong>Nothing places and nothing selects</strong> while you are in it, which is what makes it safe to shove the page around with your whole hand. Leave Pan and the score is pinned again.</p>\n<p><code>h</code> switches to Pan, the switch switches back. A mouse wheel scrolls in every mode.</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-out\">Getting out</h2>\n<p><code>Esc</code> is the general way back: it clears a listening favorite slot, then an armed cursor, then a paste, then a selection, and finally toggles between Select and Place. Press it enough times and you are in a known state.</p>",
    text: "Compose has exactly three modes. The switch on the Controls rail shows the two you choose between; Place is where you are when neither is lit. !The three modes and the ways between them. Place The default, and where you spend your time. A value is armed on the Notes rail, and a tap on the staff places it. - A tap on empty staff or a rest places the armed value at the nearest slot and pitch. - A tap on a notehead grabs it at once — it selects, and a vertical drag re-pitches it by staff step, sounding each one. The release commits. The mode and the armed value are untouched, so the next tap elsewhere still places. - A tap at an existing note's slot but a different pitch joins the chord. - A long press on empty staff draws a marquee, so you can select without leaving Place. - Hold two seconds in one spot and the Favorites palette comes to your hand. Select Reached by the switch, by v, by Esc, or by tapping the armed duration on the palette a second time — which clears the arming, and clearing the arming is Select. There is no armed value, so nothing places. - A tap selects a note, a rest, a whole chord by its stem, or a mark; a second tap toggles it. - A pointer down on empty staff that moves draws a freehand lasso; lifting selects everything inside it. - The same grab-and-drag on a notehead works here too. - A tap on empty staff that never moves clears the selection. With a selection, every palette and rail button acts on it instead of arming. That is the one rule behind every button in Compose — see selecting, moving, deleting. Pan The hand. One finger or the pen pans; two fingers pinch to zoom. Nothing places and nothing selects while you are in it, which is what makes it safe to shove the page around with your whole hand. Leave Pan and the score is pinned again. h switches to Pan, the switch switches back. A mouse wheel scrolls in every mode. Getting out Esc is the general way back: it clears a listening favorite slot, then an armed cursor, then a paste, then a selection, and finally toggles between Select and Place. Press it enough times and you are in a known state." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "selection", title: "Selecting, moving, deleting", section: "editor", order: 3,
    summary: "Tap, lasso, drag a cluster, retype, copy and paste — and the all-or-nothing rule.",
    keywords: ["select","selection","lasso","drag","move","delete","copy","cut","paste","clipboard","retype","transpose","cluster"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Selecting","id":"selecting"},{"level":2,"text":"Moving","id":"moving"},{"level":2,"text":"Retyping","id":"retyping"},{"level":2,"text":"Deleting","id":"deleting"},{"level":2,"text":"Copy, cut, paste","id":"copy-cut-paste"}],
    html: "<h2 id=\"selecting\">Selecting</h2>\n<p>A tap takes one thing: a notehead, a rest, a whole chord by its stem, a dynamic, a text, a hairpin. Tap more to add, tap again to drop. A tap on empty staff clears everything.</p>\n<p>A <strong>lasso</strong> takes many: in Select mode, put the pointer on empty staff and draw a loop; lifting selects everything whose anchor lies inside. With <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">Gesture mode</a> on you can lasso in Place mode too, and in Place a <strong>long press</strong> draws a marquee without changing mode.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-lasso\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-lasso\">A loop takes everything inside it.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"38\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"45\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"52\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"59\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"66\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"34\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"38.4\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"38.4\" y2=\"36.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"68\" y=\"48.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"63.6\" y1=\"48.5\" x2=\"63.6\" y2=\"70.9\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"102\" y=\"55.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"106.4\" y1=\"55.5\" x2=\"106.4\" y2=\"33.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"136\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"131.6\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"131.6\" y2=\"67.4\"/><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"52,24 86,16 110,30 112,62 92,82 58,80 44,58 52,26\"/></svg><figcaption>A loop takes everything inside it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><code>←</code> and <code>→</code> move the selection to the previous or next thing in the staff; <code>Shift</code> with them extends it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"moving\">Moving</h2>\n<p><strong>Pitch.</strong> Put the pen down on a selected notehead and drag up or down. It moves by staff step, sounding each one. Horizontal drags are ignored — a note's place in time belongs to its slot, not to your hand. <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> do the same thing, one step at a time.</p>\n<p><strong>A cluster.</strong> When everything selected is a notehead, grabbing any one of them drags them <strong>all</strong> by the same number of steps, sounding the moving cluster. If one of them would collide or run off the staff, the whole cluster holds — it never half-moves.</p>\n<p><strong>A mark.</strong> A dynamic, a text or a hairpin drags sideways along the staff, slot by slot, across barlines and system breaks. <code>←</code> and <code>→</code> nudge it; <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> move it away from the staff. A selected hairpin shows two end handles: the body drags both ends, a handle drags one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retyping\">Retyping</h2>\n<p>With a selection, tap a duration and every selected note becomes that value — and that value is armed for the next placement too. Dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals, articulations and dynamics all work the same way: with a selection they act on it, without one they arm.</p>\n<p><strong>It is all or nothing.</strong> If any one of the notes cannot fit at the new value, that bar flashes and <strong>nothing changes anywhere</strong>. Never truncated, never pushed into the next bar. A tie across the barline is the honest way to cross one, and it is a single tap.</p>\n<p>A selection that holds rests is refused with a hint. Rests are the gaps, not things to retype.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deleting\">Deleting</h2>\n<p><code>Delete</code> or <code>Backspace</code>, or the bin on the Controls rail. What went becomes a rest, because a bar always adds up. With <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">Gesture mode</a> on, a stroke through <em>selected</em> notes deletes them too.</p>\n<h2 id=\"copy-cut-paste\">Copy, cut, paste</h2>\n<p><strong>Copy</strong> takes the selection as a phrase: each note and rest with its value, its pitches, its offset from the earliest onset, and its staff relative to the topmost. A partial chord copies only the pitches you picked. The clipboard lives for the session and travels between compositions. <strong>Cut</strong> is copy and delete.</p>\n<p><strong>Paste arms a cursor.</strong> A ghost of the whole phrase follows the pen, snapped to the grid of its first value. Tap to drop; the staff you tap becomes the phrase's top staff. The drop <strong>replaces</strong> what is under it, appends bars past the end, and refuses a note that would straddle a barline — the bar flashes and the cursor stays armed. What you pasted stays selected, so one cluster drag transposes it all.</p>\n<p><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> + <code>C</code>, <code>X</code>, <code>V</code> on a desk.</p>",
    text: "Selecting A tap takes one thing: a notehead, a rest, a whole chord by its stem, a dynamic, a text, a hairpin. Tap more to add, tap again to drop. A tap on empty staff clears everything. A lasso takes many: in Select mode, put the pointer on empty staff and draw a loop; lifting selects everything whose anchor lies inside. With Gesture mode on you can lasso in Place mode too, and in Place a long press draws a marquee without changing mode. !A loop takes everything inside it. ← and → move the selection to the previous or next thing in the staff; Shift with them extends it. Moving Pitch. Put the pen down on a selected notehead and drag up or down. It moves by staff step, sounding each one. Horizontal drags are ignored — a note's place in time belongs to its slot, not to your hand. ↑ and ↓ do the same thing, one step at a time. A cluster. When everything selected is a notehead, grabbing any one of them drags them all by the same number of steps, sounding the moving cluster. If one of them would collide or run off the staff, the whole cluster holds — it never half-moves. A mark. A dynamic, a text or a hairpin drags sideways along the staff, slot by slot, across barlines and system breaks. ← and → nudge it; ↑ and ↓ move it away from the staff. A selected hairpin shows two end handles: the body drags both ends, a handle drags one. Retyping With a selection, tap a duration and every selected note becomes that value — and that value is armed for the next placement too. Dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals, articulations and dynamics all work the same way: with a selection they act on it, without one they arm. It is all or nothing. If any one of the notes cannot fit at the new value, that bar flashes and nothing changes anywhere. Never truncated, never pushed into the next bar. A tie across the barline is the honest way to cross one, and it is a single tap. A selection that holds rests is refused with a hint. Rests are the gaps, not things to retype. Deleting Delete or Backspace, or the bin on the Controls rail. What went becomes a rest, because a bar always adds up. With Gesture mode on, a stroke through selected notes deletes them too. Copy, cut, paste Copy takes the selection as a phrase: each note and rest with its value, its pitches, its offset from the earliest onset, and its staff relative to the topmost. A partial chord copies only the pitches you picked. The clipboard lives for the session and travels between compositions. Cut is copy and delete. Paste arms a cursor. A ghost of the whole phrase follows the pen, snapped to the grid of its first value. Tap to drop; the staff you tap becomes the phrase's top staff. The drop replaces what is under it, appends bars past the end, and refuses a note that would straddle a barline — the bar flashes and the cursor stays armed. What you pasted stays selected, so one cluster drag transposes it all. ⌘/Ctrl + C, X, V on a desk." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "input", title: "Pen and Touch", section: "editor", order: 4,
    summary: "Which contacts the score listens to, and how to write with a finger when the Pencil dies.",
    keywords: ["pen","pencil","touch","finger","palm","stylus","apple pencil","aim","input","ipad"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Pen","id":"pen"},{"level":2,"text":"Touch","id":"touch"},{"level":2,"text":"Hold, then slide, to aim","id":"hold-then-slide-to-aim"},{"level":2,"text":"Which one you start in","id":"which-one-you-start-in"}],
    html: "<p>A resting palm must never write a note. That rule shapes everything here. <strong>Pen ∣ Touch</strong> lives in <a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">Options ▾</a> under <em>Input</em>, and it exists only on a device that can be touched — a desktop never sees it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pen\">Pen</h2>\n<p>The Pencil, or a mouse, draws. <strong>A finger on the score does nothing at all</strong> — no ghost, no tap, no grab, no lasso. It cannot interrupt a stroke in flight, and it cannot place a stray note while your hand rests on the glass.</p>\n<p>This is deliberately absolute. An earlier version let a quick, narrow finger tap through, and that loophole is exactly where a palm got in.</p>\n<p>The rails are separate from the score, so your fingers keep working on every button in every setting. In Pan, one finger still pans and two still pinch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"touch\">Touch</h2>\n<p>The finger becomes the pen. Use it when the Pencil is dead, or on a phone.</p>\n<ul><li>A <strong>tap places</strong>, however slowly you take it.</li><li>In <strong>Select</strong> a finger grabs a note, rest or mark from a fingertip away — the hit targets grow to match a finger, and the nearest thing wins. In <strong>Place</strong> the staff is for placing, so a finger grabs a head only when it lands right on it; a fat target there would grab the note you are trying to add a third above.</li><li>A <strong>second finger lets go</strong>. It is the natural cancel, and a pinch never draws.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"hold-then-slide-to-aim\">Hold, then slide, to aim</h2>\n<p>A fingertip covers three staff spaces, so a plain tap is a blunt instrument for pitch. Hold for half a second, or slide, and the gesture becomes an <strong>aim</strong>: the ghost lifts clear of your fingertip where you can actually see it, follows your finger, and lands where the ghost is when you lift.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-aim\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-aim\">Hold or slide and the ghost lifts above the fingertip.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"34\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"41\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"48\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"55\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-ghost\" x=\"120\" y=\"55\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-ghost hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"124.4\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"124.4\" y2=\"32.6\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-stroke hp-fig-dash\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"65\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-finger\" cx=\"120\" cy=\"100\" r=\"11\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"172\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"start\">the ghost lifts</text><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"120\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hold, then slide to aim</text></svg><figcaption>Hold or slide and the ghost lifts above the fingertip.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Aiming works for everything the editor places — a note, an armed key or clef, a dynamic, a fingering, a paste. Lifting off the staff, a second finger, or a cancelled pointer all abandon it. And a finger that has not slid by <strong>two seconds</strong> is not aiming, it is summoning <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">Favorites</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"which-one-you-start-in\">Which one you start in</h2>\n<p>A device that has never seen a pen starts in <strong>Touch</strong>, so a phone works from the first tap. The first time a Pencil touches the score, the switch flips to <strong>Pen</strong> once with a note about it. After that the setting is yours and never moves on its own — a deliberate Touch is not undone by the next pencil stroke.</p>\n<p>The choice is remembered per device, like your rails and your zoom.</p>",
    text: "A resting palm must never write a note. That rule shapes everything here. Pen ∣ Touch lives in Options ▾ under Input, and it exists only on a device that can be touched — a desktop never sees it. Pen The Pencil, or a mouse, draws. A finger on the score does nothing at all — no ghost, no tap, no grab, no lasso. It cannot interrupt a stroke in flight, and it cannot place a stray note while your hand rests on the glass. This is deliberately absolute. An earlier version let a quick, narrow finger tap through, and that loophole is exactly where a palm got in. The rails are separate from the score, so your fingers keep working on every button in every setting. In Pan, one finger still pans and two still pinch. Touch The finger becomes the pen. Use it when the Pencil is dead, or on a phone. - A tap places, however slowly you take it. - In Select a finger grabs a note, rest or mark from a fingertip away — the hit targets grow to match a finger, and the nearest thing wins. In Place the staff is for placing, so a finger grabs a head only when it lands right on it; a fat target there would grab the note you are trying to add a third above. - A second finger lets go. It is the natural cancel, and a pinch never draws. Hold, then slide, to aim A fingertip covers three staff spaces, so a plain tap is a blunt instrument for pitch. Hold for half a second, or slide, and the gesture becomes an aim: the ghost lifts clear of your fingertip where you can actually see it, follows your finger, and lands where the ghost is when you lift. !Hold or slide and the ghost lifts above the fingertip. Aiming works for everything the editor places — a note, an armed key or clef, a dynamic, a fingering, a paste. Lifting off the staff, a second finger, or a cancelled pointer all abandon it. And a finger that has not slid by two seconds is not aiming, it is summoning Favorites. Which one you start in A device that has never seen a pen starts in Touch, so a phone works from the first tap. The first time a Pencil touches the score, the switch flips to Pen once with a note about it. After that the setting is yours and never moves on its own — a deliberate Touch is not undone by the next pencil stroke. The choice is remembered per device, like your rails and your zoom." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "gestures", title: "Gesture mode", section: "editor", order: 5,
    summary: "Six strokes that save a trip to Select — lasso, strike, four chevrons, and the hold.",
    keywords: ["gesture","gestures","chevron","arrowhead","lasso","strike","stroke","shape","hold","delete","accidental"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Drag to lasso","id":"drag-to-lasso"},{"level":2,"text":"Strike to delete","id":"strike-to-delete"},{"level":2,"text":"The four chevrons","id":"the-four-chevrons"},{"level":3,"text":"› and ‹ — the value","id":"and-the-value"},{"level":3,"text":"∧ and ∨ — the accidental","id":"and-the-accidental"},{"level":2,"text":"Hold two seconds","id":"hold-two-seconds"},{"level":2,"text":"What wins","id":"what-wins"}],
    html: "<p>Gesture mode is <strong>off until you turn it on</strong> — a lasso appearing mid-placement is new behaviour, so a device opts in. The switch is in <a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">Options ▾</a> under <em>Input</em>, and it is remembered per device.</p>\n<p>With it on, the common moves stop needing a trip to Select. Everything below works in <strong>Place and Select</strong>, drawn with a Pencil, a mouse, or — in Touch mode — a finger that <strong>slides at once</strong> (a finger that holds first is aiming, not drawing).</p>\n<h2 id=\"drag-to-lasso\">Drag to lasso</h2>\n<p>A stroke on empty staff draws a freehand path under the tip. Lifting closes it and selects everything inside. A stroke that never travelled is still the tap it always was, so plain placing is untouched.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-lasso\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-lasso\">A loop takes everything inside it.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"38\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"45\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"52\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"59\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"66\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"34\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"38.4\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"38.4\" y2=\"36.6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"68\" y=\"48.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"63.6\" y1=\"48.5\" x2=\"63.6\" y2=\"70.9\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"102\" y=\"55.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"106.4\" y1=\"55.5\" x2=\"106.4\" y2=\"33.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"136\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"131.6\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"131.6\" y2=\"67.4\"/><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"52,24 86,16 110,30 112,62 92,82 58,80 44,58 52,26\"/></svg><figcaption>A loop takes everything inside it.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"strike-to-delete\">Strike to delete</h2>\n<p>A line drawn through <strong>selected</strong> noteheads or dynamics deletes them, in one undo step. Rests come back in their place, as always.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-strike\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-strike\">A line through selected notes deletes them.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"38\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"45\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"52\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"59\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"14\" y1=\"66\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"66\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"34\" y=\"59\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"38.4\" y1=\"59\" x2=\"38.4\" y2=\"36.6\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-halo\" cx=\"68\" cy=\"48.5\" r=\"9.5\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"68\" y=\"48.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"63.6\" y1=\"48.5\" x2=\"63.6\" y2=\"70.9\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-halo\" cx=\"102\" cy=\"55.5\" r=\"9.5\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"102\" y=\"55.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"106.4\" y1=\"55.5\" x2=\"106.4\" y2=\"33.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"136\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"131.6\" y1=\"45\" x2=\"131.6\" y2=\"67.4\"/><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"56,66 92,44 126,52\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"126,52 120.6,53.2 121.7,48.5\"/></svg><figcaption>A line through selected notes deletes them.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Only <em>selected</em> things can be struck. That is the safety: a lasso can never delete, and a stroke across notes you have not chosen selects nothing and takes nothing. A stroke counts as a line — rather than a loop — when it ends far from where it started, or encloses almost no area.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-four-chevrons\">The four chevrons</h2>\n<p>A compass. One axis for <strong>time</strong>, one for <strong>pitch spelling</strong>. Which way you drew it does not matter, and no stroke can read as two shapes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"and-the-value\">› and ‹ — the value</h3>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-chevron-right\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-chevron-right\">A right chevron takes the next shortest value.</title><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"26,30 60,62 26,94\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"34\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"41\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"48\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"55\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"114\" y=\"51.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"118.4\" y1=\"51.5\" x2=\"118.4\" y2=\"29.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-arrowword\" x=\"150\" y=\"55.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">→</text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"188\" y=\"51.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"192.4\" y1=\"51.5\" x2=\"192.4\" y2=\"29.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"192.4\" y=\"29.1\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\"></text></svg><figcaption>A right chevron takes the next shortest value.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-chevron-left\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-chevron-left\">A left chevron takes the next longest.</title><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"60,30 26,62 60,94\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"34\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"41\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"48\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"55\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"88\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"114\" y=\"51.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"118.4\" y1=\"51.5\" x2=\"118.4\" y2=\"29.1\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"118.4\" y=\"29.1\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"start\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-arrowword\" x=\"150\" y=\"55.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">→</text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"188\" y=\"51.5\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"192.4\" y1=\"51.5\" x2=\"192.4\" y2=\"29.1\"/></svg><figcaption>A left chevron takes the next longest.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><strong>›</strong> is shorter, <strong>‹</strong> is longer — on the palette the shorter values sit to the right, so the sign points along the rail. The ladder runs double whole · whole · half · quarter · eighth · sixteenth · 32nd · 64th, the main row and the ▾ row together.</p>\n<p><strong>With notes selected</strong>, each note steps one rung <strong>from its own value</strong>, keeping its dots, exactly as if you had tapped that button on the rail — so a sixteenth stepped longer becomes an eighth and eats the sixteenth rest beside it. A note already at the end of the ladder stays while the others move; only when none can move do you get a nudge. <strong>With nothing selected</strong> the armed value steps instead.</p>\n<h3 id=\"and-the-accidental\">∧ and ∨ — the accidental</h3>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-chevron-up\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-chevron-up\">An up chevron raises the accidental.</title><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"26,82 46,42 66,82\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"34\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"41\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"48\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"55\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"122\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"126.4\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"126.4\" y2=\"46.6\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"114.3\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"129.7\" y2=\"69\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-arrowword\" x=\"158\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\">→</text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"184.7\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"198\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"202.4\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"202.4\" y2=\"46.6\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"190.3\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"205.7\" y2=\"69\"/></svg><figcaption>An up chevron raises the accidental.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-chevron-down\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-chevron-down\">A down chevron lowers it.</title><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"26,42 46,82 66,42\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"34\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"41\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"41\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"48\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"55\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"55\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"92\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"226\" y2=\"62\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"108.7\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"122\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"126.4\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"126.4\" y2=\"46.6\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"114.3\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"129.7\" y2=\"69\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-arrowword\" x=\"158\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\">→</text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"184.7\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"198\" y=\"69\" font-size=\"28\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><line class=\"hp-fig-ink hp-fig-stem\" x1=\"202.4\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"202.4\" y2=\"46.6\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"190.3\" y1=\"69\" x2=\"205.7\" y2=\"69\"/></svg><figcaption>A down chevron lowers it.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Every selected pitch moves one step: ♭♭ · ♭ · ♮ · ♯ · ♯♯. The <strong>letter never changes</strong> — a C♯♯ stays a C♯♯, it never becomes a D — and the engraver decides what is actually drawn, so a sign appears only where the pitch differs from what is in force. No courtesy accidentals are invented; the ♮ on the Notes rail is still how you ask for one on purpose.</p>\n<p>Each pitch steps from its own alteration, so a chord of C♯ and E♭ becomes C♯♯ and E♮ in one stroke. <strong>With nothing selected</strong> the chevrons step the one-shot armed accidental instead, and the next note you place carries it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hold-two-seconds\">Hold two seconds</h2>\n<p>In Place mode, holding in one spot without travelling summons the <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">Favorites palette</a> to your hand — or moves it there if it is already out.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-hold\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-hold\">Hold two seconds and Favorites comes to your hand.</title><circle class=\"hp-fig-ring\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"26\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-ring hp-fig-dash\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"17\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-dot\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"62\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hold 2 s</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel\" x=\"118\" y=\"30\" width=\"104\" height=\"76\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel-bar\" x=\"118\" y=\"30\" width=\"104\" height=\"14\" rx=\"0\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"126\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"126\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"158\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"158\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"190\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"190\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/></svg><figcaption>Hold two seconds and Favorites comes to your hand.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"what-wins\">What wins</h2>\n<p>At the lift, Compose reads the stroke in this order: <strong>chevron, then strike, then lasso</strong>. A deliberate shape beats a strike, so an ∧ drawn across your selected notes edits them rather than crossing them out.</p>\n<p>A grab always starts on a notehead and a gesture always starts on empty staff, so dragging a cluster and striking through it can never collide.</p>\n<p>With Gesture off, every mode behaves exactly as it did before.</p>",
    text: "Gesture mode is off until you turn it on — a lasso appearing mid-placement is new behaviour, so a device opts in. The switch is in Options ▾ under Input, and it is remembered per device. With it on, the common moves stop needing a trip to Select. Everything below works in Place and Select, drawn with a Pencil, a mouse, or — in Touch mode — a finger that slides at once (a finger that holds first is aiming, not drawing). Drag to lasso A stroke on empty staff draws a freehand path under the tip. Lifting closes it and selects everything inside. A stroke that never travelled is still the tap it always was, so plain placing is untouched. !A loop takes everything inside it. Strike to delete A line drawn through selected noteheads or dynamics deletes them, in one undo step. Rests come back in their place, as always. !A line through selected notes deletes them. Only selected things can be struck. That is the safety: a lasso can never delete, and a stroke across notes you have not chosen selects nothing and takes nothing. A stroke counts as a line — rather than a loop — when it ends far from where it started, or encloses almost no area. The four chevrons A compass. One axis for time, one for pitch spelling. Which way you drew it does not matter, and no stroke can read as two shapes. › and ‹ — the value !A right chevron takes the next shortest value. !A left chevron takes the next longest. › is shorter, ‹ is longer — on the palette the shorter values sit to the right, so the sign points along the rail. The ladder runs double whole · whole · half · quarter · eighth · sixteenth · 32nd · 64th, the main row and the ▾ row together. With notes selected, each note steps one rung from its own value, keeping its dots, exactly as if you had tapped that button on the rail — so a sixteenth stepped longer becomes an eighth and eats the sixteenth rest beside it. A note already at the end of the ladder stays while the others move; only when none can move do you get a nudge. With nothing selected the armed value steps instead. ∧ and ∨ — the accidental !An up chevron raises the accidental. !A down chevron lowers it. Every selected pitch moves one step: ♭♭ · ♭ · ♮ · ♯ · ♯♯. The letter never changes — a C♯♯ stays a C♯♯, it never becomes a D — and the engraver decides what is actually drawn, so a sign appears only where the pitch differs from what is in force. No courtesy accidentals are invented; the ♮ on the Notes rail is still how you ask for one on purpose. Each pitch steps from its own alteration, so a chord of C♯ and E♭ becomes C♯♯ and E♮ in one stroke. With nothing selected the chevrons step the one-shot armed accidental instead, and the next note you place carries it. Hold two seconds In Place mode, holding in one spot without travelling summons the Favorites palette to your hand — or moves it there if it is already out. !Hold two seconds and Favorites comes to your hand. What wins At the lift, Compose reads the stroke in this order: chevron, then strike, then lasso. A deliberate shape beats a strike, so an ∧ drawn across your selected notes edits them rather than crossing them out. A grab always starts on a notehead and a gesture always starts on empty staff, so dragging a cluster and striking through it can never collide. With Gesture off, every mode behaves exactly as it did before." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "favorites", title: "The Favorites palette", section: "editor", order: 6,
    summary: "A floating panel of the buttons you actually use, summoned to your hand by a hold.",
    keywords: ["favorites","favourite","palette","panel","popup","shortcut","hold","summon","pages","slots"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Summoning it","id":"summoning-it"},{"level":2,"text":"Living with it","id":"living-with-it"},{"level":2,"text":"Putting a button in a slot","id":"putting-a-button-in-a-slot"},{"level":2,"text":"Clearing one","id":"clearing-one"},{"level":2,"text":"What is remembered","id":"what-is-remembered"}],
    html: "<p>Favorites is a shortcut layer and nothing more. A favorite <strong>is</strong> a rail button — tapping it is tapping that button, with the same arming, the same behaviour on a selection, the same toast. It even shows the button's state, so a favorite of the quarter note lights up when the quarter is armed.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/favorites-panel.png\" alt=\"The Favorites panel: six slots, eight pages, a grabber and an ×.\" width=\"370\" height=\"414\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Favorites panel: six slots, eight pages, a grabber and an ×.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"summoning-it\">Summoning it</h2>\n<p>In <strong>Place mode</strong>, hold the pen — or a finger, in Touch mode — in one spot for <strong>two seconds</strong> without travelling. The panel opens with its grabber under your pointer, and the hold does not place anything. If it is already out, the hold <strong>moves it to your hand</strong>. One motion pulls the palette to where you are working.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-gesture-hold\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-gesture-hold\">Hold two seconds in one spot.</title><circle class=\"hp-fig-ring\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"26\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-ring hp-fig-dash\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"17\"/><circle class=\"hp-fig-dot\" cx=\"62\" cy=\"66\" r=\"6\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"62\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\">hold 2 s</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel\" x=\"118\" y=\"30\" width=\"104\" height=\"76\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel-bar\" x=\"118\" y=\"30\" width=\"104\" height=\"14\" rx=\"0\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"126\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"126\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"158\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"158\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"190\" y=\"50\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"190\" y=\"76\" width=\"26\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\"/></svg><figcaption>Hold two seconds in one spot.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The hold only works in Place — Select and Pan are not for placing — and never on a notehead, because that is a grab. A Pen-mode finger is a palm and summons nothing.</p>\n<p>You can also switch it on and off from <a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">Options ▾</a> under <em>Input</em>, which shows it at wherever you last left it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"living-with-it\">Living with it</h2>\n<ul><li>The <strong>grabber bar</strong> at the top drags it anywhere, with pen or finger; it is clamped on screen and the place is remembered.</li><li>The <strong>×</strong> hides it. Tapping the score does <strong>not</strong> — it stays out while you write, which is the point.</li><li>Six slots in a 3 × 2 grid, and <strong>eight pages</strong> behind the ◀ <em>1 / 8</em> ▶ footer. Forty-eight slots, and the page you were on is remembered.</li></ul>\n<p>Page 1 arrives seeded with ♯, ♭, ♮, dot, tie and tuplet. Pages 2–8 start empty.</p>\n<h2 id=\"putting-a-button-in-a-slot\">Putting a button in a slot</h2>\n<ol><li>Tap an <strong>empty slot</strong>. It starts listening — a pulsing outline, and the hint says so.</li><li>Tap <strong>any rail button</strong>. It is captured into the slot rather than fired.</li><li>Rows inside hold menus count: open the menu by hold or by its ▾, then tap <em>mp</em>, <em>ffff</em>, a quintuplet, the dashed <em>cresc.</em> — whatever you actually want.</li></ol>\n<p>Tapping the listening slot again, another slot, or the × cancels. The same button may sit in as many slots as you like.</p>\n<p><strong>What cannot be a favorite</strong>, with a toast saying so: anything on the header, Controls or Transport rails — File, Options, undo, redo, the modes, Pen ∣ Touch, Gesture, Favorites itself, the clipboard, zoom, play, tempo — a menu opener, and the <em>set</em> button of the text box, which needs the words you typed. Everything on the six palette rails is fair game.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clearing-one\">Clearing one</h2>\n<p>Hold a filled slot for a second and a half. It empties, with a tick and a <em>removed</em>. A plain tap fires it instead; a hold on an empty slot does nothing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-is-remembered\">What is remembered</h2>\n<p>All of it — the pages, the slots, the page you are on, the position, whether it is showing — <strong>per device</strong>. Favorites are deliberately not synced: a favorite names a rail button, and rails are a device's habit, like which ones you show.</p>",
    text: "Favorites is a shortcut layer and nothing more. A favorite is a rail button — tapping it is tapping that button, with the same arming, the same behaviour on a selection, the same toast. It even shows the button's state, so a favorite of the quarter note lights up when the quarter is armed. !The Favorites panel: six slots, eight pages, a grabber and an ×. Summoning it In Place mode, hold the pen — or a finger, in Touch mode — in one spot for two seconds without travelling. The panel opens with its grabber under your pointer, and the hold does not place anything. If it is already out, the hold moves it to your hand. One motion pulls the palette to where you are working. !Hold two seconds in one spot. The hold only works in Place — Select and Pan are not for placing — and never on a notehead, because that is a grab. A Pen-mode finger is a palm and summons nothing. You can also switch it on and off from Options ▾ under Input, which shows it at wherever you last left it. Living with it - The grabber bar at the top drags it anywhere, with pen or finger; it is clamped on screen and the place is remembered. - The × hides it. Tapping the score does not — it stays out while you write, which is the point. - Six slots in a 3 × 2 grid, and eight pages behind the ◀ 1 / 8 ▶ footer. Forty-eight slots, and the page you were on is remembered. Page 1 arrives seeded with ♯, ♭, ♮, dot, tie and tuplet. Pages 2–8 start empty. Putting a button in a slot 1. Tap an empty slot. It starts listening — a pulsing outline, and the hint says so. 2. Tap any rail button. It is captured into the slot rather than fired. 3. Rows inside hold menus count: open the menu by hold or by its ▾, then tap mp, ffff, a quintuplet, the dashed cresc. — whatever you actually want. Tapping the listening slot again, another slot, or the × cancels. The same button may sit in as many slots as you like. What cannot be a favorite, with a toast saying so: anything on the header, Controls or Transport rails — File, Options, undo, redo, the modes, Pen ∣ Touch, Gesture, Favorites itself, the clipboard, zoom, play, tempo — a menu opener, and the set button of the text box, which needs the words you typed. Everything on the six palette rails is fair game. Clearing one Hold a filled slot for a second and a half. It empties, with a tick and a removed. A plain tap fires it instead; a hold on an empty slot does nothing. What is remembered All of it — the pages, the slots, the page you are on, the position, whether it is showing — per device. Favorites are deliberately not synced: a favorite names a rail button, and rails are a device's habit, like which ones you show." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "options", title: "The Options panel", section: "editor", order: 7,
    summary: "The header's control panel — what draws, gestures, favorites, which rails show, and help.",
    keywords: ["options","settings","panel","rails","show","hide","toggle","preferences","control panel"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Input","id":"input"},{"level":2,"text":"Rails","id":"rails"},{"level":2,"text":"Help","id":"help"}],
    html: "<p><strong>Options ▾</strong> at the right of the header is Compose's control panel. It has three captioned sections, and it <strong>stays open while you flip things</strong>, so you can turn on two rails and a gesture in one visit.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/options-panel.png\" alt=\"The Options panel: Input, Rails and Help.\" width=\"718\" height=\"1204\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Options panel: Input, Rails and Help.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"input\">Input</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Pen ∣ Touch</strong> — which contacts the score listens to. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/input\">Pen and Touch</a>. It is absent on a device with no touch screen.</li><li><strong>Gesture</strong> — lasso, strike and the chevrons. Off by default. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">Gesture mode</a>.</li><li><strong>Favorites</strong> — show or hide the floating palette. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">the Favorites palette</a>.</li></ul>\n<p>The rows keep their quiet ink even when they are on; the pill at the right carries the state. The accent is reserved for what the <em>next tap</em> does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rails\">Rails</h2>\n<p>A checklist of the eight rails. Three are on to start with — Controls, Transport and Notes — and the other five are one tick away:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>rail</th><th>what is on it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-controls\">Controls</a></td><td>undo, redo, Select ∣ Pan, the clipboard, zoom</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-transport\">Transport</a></td><td>play, seek, tempo</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-notes\">Notes</a></td><td>voices, durations, dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-keys\">Key · time · clef · marks</a></td><td>signatures, clefs, articulations, ornaments, slurs</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-dynamics\">Dynamics · hairpins · text</a></td><td>everything that sits on a beat</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">Form</a></td><td>barlines, repeats, endings, jumps, tempo marks, bars</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-piano\">Piano</a></td><td>pedal, octave lines, fingering</td></tr><tr><td><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-marks\">Grace · tremolo · marks</a></td><td>grace notes, tremolo, trills, engraving overrides</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every rail you show costs the score a lane of height, so most people keep two or three out and turn the others on when they need them. Your choice is remembered per device.</p>\n<h2 id=\"help\">Help</h2>\n<p>One row, which opens this help. It is also on the compositions list as a <code>?</code>, and <code>?</code> on a keyboard opens it from the editor.</p>",
    text: "Options ▾ at the right of the header is Compose's control panel. It has three captioned sections, and it stays open while you flip things, so you can turn on two rails and a gesture in one visit. !The Options panel: Input, Rails and Help. Input - Pen ∣ Touch — which contacts the score listens to. See Pen and Touch. It is absent on a device with no touch screen. - Gesture — lasso, strike and the chevrons. Off by default. See Gesture mode. - Favorites — show or hide the floating palette. See the Favorites palette. The rows keep their quiet ink even when they are on; the pill at the right carries the state. The accent is reserved for what the next tap does. Rails A checklist of the eight rails. Three are on to start with — Controls, Transport and Notes — and the other five are one tick away: rail what is on it --- --- Controls undo, redo, Select ∣ Pan, the clipboard, zoom Transport play, seek, tempo Notes voices, durations, dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals Key · time · clef · marks signatures, clefs, articulations, ornaments, slurs Dynamics · hairpins · text everything that sits on a beat Form barlines, repeats, endings, jumps, tempo marks, bars Piano pedal, octave lines, fingering Grace · tremolo · marks grace notes, tremolo, trills, engraving overrides Every rail you show costs the score a lane of height, so most people keep two or three out and turn the others on when they need them. Your choice is remembered per device. Help One row, which opens this help. It is also on the compositions list as a ?, and ? on a keyboard opens it from the editor." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "shortcuts", title: "Keyboard shortcuts", section: "editor", order: 8,
    summary: "Every key Compose listens to, for when there is a keyboard attached.",
    keywords: ["keyboard","shortcut","shortcuts","keys","hotkey","command","control","space","escape","delete"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Values","id":"values"},{"level":2,"text":"Moving and choosing","id":"moving-and-choosing"},{"level":2,"text":"Modes","id":"modes"},{"level":2,"text":"Clipboard and history","id":"clipboard-and-history"},{"level":2,"text":"Voices and playback","id":"voices-and-playback"},{"level":2,"text":"Help","id":"help"}],
    html: "<p><code>⌘</code> on a Mac or an iPad with a keyboard, <code>Ctrl</code> elsewhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"values\">Values</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>1</code> – <code>7</code></td><td>64th, 32nd, sixteenth, eighth, quarter, half, whole</td></tr><tr><td><code>.</code></td><td>dot — cycles none · dotted · double dotted</td></tr><tr><td><code>t</code></td><td>tie</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>With a selection these retype it; with nothing selected they arm. That is <a href=\"#/compose/help/rails\">the one rule</a> every button follows.</p>\n<h2 id=\"moving-and-choosing\">Moving and choosing</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>←</code> <code>→</code></td><td>the previous / next thing in the staff</td></tr><tr><td><code>Shift</code> <code>←</code> <code>→</code></td><td>extend the selection</td></tr><tr><td><code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>re-pitch by a staff step — or nudge a rest, or move a mark away from the staff</td></tr><tr><td><code>←</code> <code>→</code> on a mark</td><td>slide it a slot earlier or later</td></tr><tr><td><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>⇧</code> <code>↑</code> <code>↓</code></td><td>send the note to the other staff (cross-staff)</td></tr><tr><td><code>Delete</code> <code>Backspace</code></td><td>delete — a rest takes its place</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"modes\">Modes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>v</code></td><td>Select</td></tr><tr><td><code>h</code></td><td>Pan</td></tr><tr><td><code>Esc</code></td><td>step back out: a listening favorite slot, an armed cursor, a paste, a selection, then Select ⇄ Place</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"clipboard-and-history\">Clipboard and history</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>C</code> <code>X</code> <code>V</code></td><td>copy, cut, paste — paste arms a cursor, then tap where it goes</td></tr><tr><td><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>Z</code></td><td>undo</td></tr><tr><td><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>⇧</code> <code>Z</code></td><td>redo</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"voices-and-playback\">Voices and playback</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>1</code> – <code>4</code></td><td>the active voice</td></tr><tr><td><code>Space</code></td><td>play / pause</td></tr><tr><td><code>Home</code></td><td>stop</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"help\">Help</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>key</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>?</code></td><td>open this help</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
    text: "⌘ on a Mac or an iPad with a keyboard, Ctrl elsewhere. Values key does --- --- 1 – 7 64th, 32nd, sixteenth, eighth, quarter, half, whole . dot — cycles none · dotted · double dotted t tie With a selection these retype it; with nothing selected they arm. That is the one rule every button follows. Moving and choosing key does --- --- ← → the previous / next thing in the staff Shift ← → extend the selection ↑ ↓ re-pitch by a staff step — or nudge a rest, or move a mark away from the staff ← → on a mark slide it a slot earlier or later ⌘/Ctrl ⇧ ↑ ↓ send the note to the other staff (cross-staff) Delete Backspace delete — a rest takes its place Modes key does --- --- v Select h Pan Esc step back out: a listening favorite slot, an armed cursor, a paste, a selection, then Select ⇄ Place Clipboard and history key does --- --- ⌘/Ctrl C X V copy, cut, paste — paste arms a cursor, then tap where it goes ⌘/Ctrl Z undo ⌘/Ctrl ⇧ Z redo Voices and playback key does --- --- ⌘/Ctrl 1 – 4 the active voice Space play / pause Home stop Help key does --- --- ? open this help" },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "nudges", title: "When an edit is refused", section: "editor", order: 9,
    summary: "The bar flashes, a sentence appears, and nothing changed — what each one means.",
    keywords: ["nudge","refused","error","flash","cannot","wont","problem","troubleshooting","too long","help"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"\"too long for this bar\"","id":"too-long-for-this-bar"},{"level":2,"text":"\"tie needs the same pitch next\"","id":"tie-needs-the-same-pitch-next"},{"level":2,"text":"\"pick a note for the accidental\"","id":"pick-a-note-for-the-accidental"},{"level":2,"text":"\"already the shortest\" · \"already the longest\" · \"already double sharp\"","id":"already-the-shortest-already-the-longest-already-double-sharp"},{"level":2,"text":"\"that beat already has a note\"","id":"that-beat-already-has-a-note"},{"level":2,"text":"\"write the pickup first\" · a pickup refused","id":"write-the-pickup-first-a-pickup-refused"},{"level":2,"text":"\"voices run 1 to 4\"","id":"voices-run-1-to-4"},{"level":2,"text":"\"that one can't be a favorite\"","id":"that-one-can-t-be-a-favorite"},{"level":2,"text":"Nothing happened at all","id":"nothing-happened-at-all"}],
    html: "<p>Compose has a standing rule: <strong>a refused edit changes nothing</strong>. Not part of it, not a truncated version of it, not the neighbours quietly rewritten to make room. The bar flashes, a short sentence appears, and the piece is exactly as it was. No undo step is spent.</p>\n<p>This is on purpose. Silently altering what you did not ask about is the class of surprise that makes a notation app untrustworthy.</p>\n<p>Here are the sentences you are most likely to meet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"too-long-for-this-bar\">&quot;too long for this bar&quot;</h2>\n<p>You asked several notes to become a longer value and at least one of them will not fit. It is <strong>all or nothing</strong>: if one cannot grow, none of them do.</p>\n<p>The way through is a <strong>tie across the barline</strong> — select the note, press <code>t</code> or the tie button, and the value continues into the next bar honestly. Or shorten something else in the bar first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tie-needs-the-same-pitch-next\">&quot;tie needs the same pitch next&quot;</h2>\n<p>A tie joins two soundings of the <em>same</em> pitch. With one note selected, Compose looks for the next note of that pitch in the same staff — across a barline is fine. If the next note is a different pitch, there is nothing to tie to. Select both notes explicitly, or fix the pitch.</p>\n<p>A slur is the other thing you may want: it is on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-keys\">the Key · time · clef · marks rail</a>, and it joins <em>different</em> pitches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pick-a-note-for-the-accidental\">&quot;pick a note for the accidental&quot;</h2>\n<p>You used ∧ or ∨ with only rests or marks selected. Accidentals belong to pitches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"already-the-shortest-already-the-longest-already-double-sharp\">&quot;already the shortest&quot; · &quot;already the longest&quot; · &quot;already double sharp&quot;</h2>\n<p>A chevron took you off the end of a ladder. Nothing to do — you are at the edge.</p>\n<h2 id=\"that-beat-already-has-a-note\">&quot;that beat already has a note&quot;</h2>\n<p>You tried to place a rest where a note is. Delete the note instead; the rest appears by itself, because a bar always adds up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"write-the-pickup-first-a-pickup-refused\">&quot;write the pickup first&quot; · a pickup refused</h2>\n<p>The <em>Pickup</em> button cuts the silence a bar already has. An <strong>empty</strong> bar has nothing to cut, a bar in the middle of a phrase is not an opening or a closing bar, and a bar whose chosen end starts with a <em>note</em> has no silence there to remove. Write the pickup's notes first, then stamp it. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">the Form rail</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"voices-run-1-to-4\">&quot;voices run 1 to 4&quot;</h2>\n<p>Four voices a staff is the limit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"that-one-can-t-be-a-favorite\">&quot;that one can't be a favorite&quot;</h2>\n<p>Some buttons are not shortcuts to anything — the modes, undo, the menus, zoom, play. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">the Favorites palette</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"nothing-happened-at-all\">Nothing happened at all</h2>\n<p>If a <strong>finger</strong> does nothing on the score, you are in <strong>Pen</strong> mode, where a finger is treated as a palm. Switch to Touch in <a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">Options</a>.</p>\n<p>If a <strong>gesture</strong> does nothing, Gesture mode is off — it is off until you turn it on.</p>\n<p>If the score <strong>scrolls instead of placing</strong>, you are in Pan. Tap Select ∣ Pan to come back.</p>",
    text: "Compose has a standing rule: a refused edit changes nothing. Not part of it, not a truncated version of it, not the neighbours quietly rewritten to make room. The bar flashes, a short sentence appears, and the piece is exactly as it was. No undo step is spent. This is on purpose. Silently altering what you did not ask about is the class of surprise that makes a notation app untrustworthy. Here are the sentences you are most likely to meet. \"too long for this bar\" You asked several notes to become a longer value and at least one of them will not fit. It is all or nothing: if one cannot grow, none of them do. The way through is a tie across the barline — select the note, press t or the tie button, and the value continues into the next bar honestly. Or shorten something else in the bar first. \"tie needs the same pitch next\" A tie joins two soundings of the same pitch. With one note selected, Compose looks for the next note of that pitch in the same staff — across a barline is fine. If the next note is a different pitch, there is nothing to tie to. Select both notes explicitly, or fix the pitch. A slur is the other thing you may want: it is on the Key · time · clef · marks rail, and it joins different pitches. \"pick a note for the accidental\" You used ∧ or ∨ with only rests or marks selected. Accidentals belong to pitches. \"already the shortest\" · \"already the longest\" · \"already double sharp\" A chevron took you off the end of a ladder. Nothing to do — you are at the edge. \"that beat already has a note\" You tried to place a rest where a note is. Delete the note instead; the rest appears by itself, because a bar always adds up. \"write the pickup first\" · a pickup refused The Pickup button cuts the silence a bar already has. An empty bar has nothing to cut, a bar in the middle of a phrase is not an opening or a closing bar, and a bar whose chosen end starts with a note has no silence there to remove. Write the pickup's notes first, then stamp it. See the Form rail. \"voices run 1 to 4\" Four voices a staff is the limit. \"that one can't be a favorite\" Some buttons are not shortcuts to anything — the modes, undo, the menus, zoom, play. See the Favorites palette. Nothing happened at all If a finger does nothing on the score, you are in Pen mode, where a finger is treated as a palm. Switch to Touch in Options. If a gesture does nothing, Gesture mode is off — it is off until you turn it on. If the score scrolls instead of placing, you are in Pan. Tap Select ∣ Pan to come back." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rails", title: "How the rails work", section: "rails", order: 1,
    summary: "One grammar behind every button on every rail — arm it, or act on the selection.",
    keywords: ["rail","rails","button","buttons","grammar","arm","armed","hold","menu","caption","lane","scroll"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"1 · Selection first, then the button","id":"1-selection-first-then-the-button"},{"level":2,"text":"2 · Some things arm a cursor instead","id":"2-some-things-arm-a-cursor-instead"},{"level":2,"text":"3 · States stick, accidentals do not","id":"3-states-stick-accidentals-do-not"},{"level":2,"text":"Hold for the variants","id":"hold-for-the-variants"},{"level":2,"text":"The lane","id":"the-lane"},{"level":2,"text":"Showing and hiding","id":"showing-and-hiding"},{"level":2,"text":"The eight","id":"the-eight"}],
    html: "<p>There are eight rails. Whichever you have showing, every button on them obeys the same three rules.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-anatomy-rail\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-anatomy-rail\">One rail: its caption, its buttons, and a corner that means \"hold for more\".</title><rect class=\"hp-fig-panel\" x=\"10\" y=\"52\" width=\"220\" height=\"34\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-cap\" x=\"26\" y=\"73\" text-anchor=\"start\">NOTES</text><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"70\" y=\"57\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"100\" y=\"57\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"130\" y=\"57\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"82\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"112\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x=\"142\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><rect class=\"hp-fig-box hp-fig-lit\" x=\"162\" y=\"57\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><polyline class=\"hp-fig-chev\" points=\"178,76 181,79 184,76\"/><rect class=\"hp-fig-box\" x=\"194\" y=\"57\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"66\" y=\"34\" text-anchor=\"start\">the caption stays put</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"60,38 42,50\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"42,50 44.8,45.3 47.4,49.2\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\">a corner chevron means: hold for more</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"168,98 176,88\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"176,88 174.8,93.4 171,90.4\"/></svg><figcaption>One rail: its caption, its buttons, and a corner that means &quot;hold for more&quot;.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"1-selection-first-then-the-button\">1 · Selection first, then the button</h2>\n<p><strong>With something selected</strong>, a button acts on it. <strong>With nothing selected</strong>, it arms the next placement.</p>\n<p>Quarter note selected, tap <em>half</em>: it is a half now, and the half is armed for your next tap. To arm without editing, tap empty staff first — that clears the selection.</p>\n<p>This one rule covers duration, dot, tie, tuplet, accidental, articulation, dynamic, everything. There is no second &quot;change this&quot; control anywhere in Compose.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-some-things-arm-a-cursor-instead\">2 · Some things arm a cursor instead</h2>\n<p>Key, time, clef, the expressions, the form marks, fingering and grace notes do not act on a selection — they are <em>placed</em>. Tap the button and it arms; a ghost follows the pen; the next tap on the staff or the bar puts it there. The same button on the same place again removes it. <code>Esc</code> or the button again disarms.</p>\n<p>Rails that work this way say so on each button: <em>&quot;then tap the bar it starts on&quot;</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-states-stick-accidentals-do-not\">3 · States stick, accidentals do not</h2>\n<p><strong>Dot</strong>, <strong>tuplet</strong> and the rest toggle are <em>states</em>: a dotted eighth stays armed until you turn the dot off. <strong>Accidentals are one-shot</strong>: ♯ arms, the next note you place carries it, and the button clears itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hold-for-the-variants\">Hold for the variants</h2>\n<p>A family of variants lives behind a <strong>hold</strong> on the button it varies, not behind another square — every rail is one lane on a phone, and squares are expensive. Hold <em>pp</em> for the extremes, <em>8va</em> for 15ma, <em>Ped.</em> for the pedal styles, the tuplet for duplets and quintuplets. A small corner chevron marks a button that has more behind it, and a <code>▾</code> opens the same menu with a tap.</p>\n<p>Rows inside those menus can be captured into <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">Favorites</a> like any other button.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-lane\">The lane</h2>\n<p>A rail is <strong>one line, always</strong>. When it does not fit it scrolls sideways under your finger; it never wraps to a second line, because a rail that grows would steal the score's height without asking. The caption at the left — <em>NOTES</em>, <em>FORM</em>, <em>DYNAMICS</em> — stays put while the buttons slide under it, so you always know which rail you are looking at.</p>\n<h2 id=\"showing-and-hiding\">Showing and hiding</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/compose/help/options\">Options ▾</a> has the checklist. Controls, Transport and Notes are on to start with. Your choice is remembered per device, and is not synced — which rails you show is a habit of the device, not of the piece.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-eight\">The eight</h2>\n<p><a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-controls\">Controls</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-transport\">Transport</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-notes\">Notes</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-keys\">Key · time · clef · marks</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-dynamics\">Dynamics · hairpins · text</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">Form</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-piano\">Piano</a> · <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-marks\">Grace · tremolo · marks</a></p>",
    text: "There are eight rails. Whichever you have showing, every button on them obeys the same three rules. !One rail: its caption, its buttons, and a corner that means \"hold for more\". 1 · Selection first, then the button With something selected, a button acts on it. With nothing selected, it arms the next placement. Quarter note selected, tap half: it is a half now, and the half is armed for your next tap. To arm without editing, tap empty staff first — that clears the selection. This one rule covers duration, dot, tie, tuplet, accidental, articulation, dynamic, everything. There is no second \"change this\" control anywhere in Compose. 2 · Some things arm a cursor instead Key, time, clef, the expressions, the form marks, fingering and grace notes do not act on a selection — they are placed. Tap the button and it arms; a ghost follows the pen; the next tap on the staff or the bar puts it there. The same button on the same place again removes it. Esc or the button again disarms. Rails that work this way say so on each button: \"then tap the bar it starts on\". 3 · States stick, accidentals do not Dot, tuplet and the rest toggle are states: a dotted eighth stays armed until you turn the dot off. Accidentals are one-shot: ♯ arms, the next note you place carries it, and the button clears itself. Hold for the variants A family of variants lives behind a hold on the button it varies, not behind another square — every rail is one lane on a phone, and squares are expensive. Hold pp for the extremes, 8va for 15ma, Ped. for the pedal styles, the tuplet for duplets and quintuplets. A small corner chevron marks a button that has more behind it, and a ▾ opens the same menu with a tap. Rows inside those menus can be captured into Favorites like any other button. The lane A rail is one line, always. When it does not fit it scrolls sideways under your finger; it never wraps to a second line, because a rail that grows would steal the score's height without asking. The caption at the left — NOTES, FORM, DYNAMICS — stays put while the buttons slide under it, so you always know which rail you are looking at. Showing and hiding Options ▾ has the checklist. Controls, Transport and Notes are on to start with. Your choice is remembered per device, and is not synced — which rails you show is a habit of the device, not of the piece. The eight Controls · Transport · Notes · Key · time · clef · marks · Dynamics · hairpins · text · Form · Piano · Grace · tremolo · marks" },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-controls", title: "The Controls rail", section: "rails", order: 2,
    summary: "Undo, redo, the mode switch, the clipboard and zoom.",
    keywords: ["controls","control","undo","redo","zoom","clipboard","copy","cut","paste","delete","bin","trash","select","pan"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Undo and redo","id":"undo-and-redo"},{"level":2,"text":"Select ∣ Pan","id":"select-pan"},{"level":2,"text":"The clipboard","id":"the-clipboard"},{"level":2,"text":"Zoom","id":"zoom"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-controls.png\" alt=\"The Controls rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Controls rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"undo-and-redo\">Undo and redo</h2>\n<p>Every committed edit is one step: a placement, a delete, a retype, the drop of a drag, a rail action, a whole drag in the Layout editor. Two hundred steps are kept. <code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>Z</code> and <code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>⇧</code> <code>Z</code>.</p>\n<p>Undo history belongs to the open editor, not to the piece — closing it and coming back starts a fresh stack. Everything up to that point is already saved.</p>\n<p>Tempo is deliberately <strong>not</strong> undoable; it is a property of the piece, like its title.</p>\n<h2 id=\"select-pan\">Select ∣ Pan</h2>\n<p>The mode switch. Neither lit is Place. See <a href=\"#/compose/help/modes\">Place, Select and Pan</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-clipboard\">The clipboard</h2>\n<p><strong>Delete</strong> (the bin) removes the selection and leaves rests. <strong>Copy</strong>, <strong>cut</strong> and <strong>paste</strong> take and drop a phrase — paste arms a cursor and the next tap drops it. <a href=\"#/compose/help/selection\">Selecting, moving, deleting</a> has the detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"zoom\">Zoom</h2>\n<p><code>−</code> and <code>+</code> step the staff size. Compose <strong>re-draws at the new size</strong> rather than scaling a picture, so glyphs stay crisp and the systems re-wrap to the width — what you see is still what will print. Your zoom is remembered per device.</p>\n<p>In Pan you can also pinch.</p>",
    text: "!The Controls rail. Undo and redo Every committed edit is one step: a placement, a delete, a retype, the drop of a drag, a rail action, a whole drag in the Layout editor. Two hundred steps are kept. ⌘/Ctrl Z and ⌘/Ctrl ⇧ Z. Undo history belongs to the open editor, not to the piece — closing it and coming back starts a fresh stack. Everything up to that point is already saved. Tempo is deliberately not undoable; it is a property of the piece, like its title. Select ∣ Pan The mode switch. Neither lit is Place. See Place, Select and Pan. The clipboard Delete (the bin) removes the selection and leaves rests. Copy, cut and paste take and drop a phrase — paste arms a cursor and the next tap drops it. Selecting, moving, deleting has the detail. Zoom − and + step the staff size. Compose re-draws at the new size rather than scaling a picture, so glyphs stay crisp and the systems re-wrap to the width — what you see is still what will print. Your zoom is remembered per device. In Pan you can also pinch." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-transport", title: "The Transport rail", section: "rails", order: 3,
    summary: "Play the piece, seek through it, and set the tempo it is written at.",
    keywords: ["transport","play","pause","stop","tempo","bpm","playback","seek","slider","playhead","rewind","forward"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Playing","id":"playing"},{"level":2,"text":"Seeking","id":"seeking"},{"level":2,"text":"What playback follows","id":"what-playback-follows"},{"level":2,"text":"Tempo","id":"tempo"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-transport.png\" alt=\"The Transport rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Transport rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"playing\">Playing</h2>\n<p><strong>Play</strong> starts from the playhead at the piece's tempo; the button becomes pause. <strong>Stop</strong> returns to the start. <code>Space</code> plays and pauses, <code>Home</code> stops.</p>\n<p>A playhead line follows the music, and the view scrolls only when the playing system leaves the screen — it will not yank the page out from under you while you are writing.</p>\n<p>You can edit while it plays; the sequence picks up your change from the current position.</p>\n<h2 id=\"seeking\">Seeking</h2>\n<p>The two arrows step a bar back and a bar forward. The slider scrubs, and reads <em>bar N of M</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-playback-follows\">What playback follows</h2>\n<p>More than the notes. Repeats, endings and the jumps unroll into passes; <em>D.S. al Coda</em> goes where it says. Tempo marks change the speed as they arrive. The pedal holds notes until it lifts. Grace notes, tremolo, trills, mordents and turns are played, fermatas are held, notes under a slur play legato, and ties merge into one sounding note.</p>\n<p>Short bars play short. What you hear is the form as written.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tempo\">Tempo</h2>\n<p><code>♩=</code> with <code>−</code> and <code>+</code>; hold either to run. Tap the number to type one. Twenty to three hundred.</p>\n<p>The tempo is <strong>saved with the piece</strong>, so it travels to your other devices and into MusicXML. It is not an undoable edit.</p>\n<p>To put a <em>written</em> tempo mark on a bar — <em>Allegro</em>, <code>♩ = 120</code>, a change partway through — use the tempo mark on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">the Form rail</a>. This rail is the speed you audition at; that one is what the paper says.</p>",
    text: "!The Transport rail. Playing Play starts from the playhead at the piece's tempo; the button becomes pause. Stop returns to the start. Space plays and pauses, Home stops. A playhead line follows the music, and the view scrolls only when the playing system leaves the screen — it will not yank the page out from under you while you are writing. You can edit while it plays; the sequence picks up your change from the current position. Seeking The two arrows step a bar back and a bar forward. The slider scrubs, and reads bar N of M. What playback follows More than the notes. Repeats, endings and the jumps unroll into passes; D.S. al Coda goes where it says. Tempo marks change the speed as they arrive. The pedal holds notes until it lifts. Grace notes, tremolo, trills, mordents and turns are played, fermatas are held, notes under a slur play legato, and ties merge into one sounding note. Short bars play short. What you hear is the form as written. Tempo ♩= with − and +; hold either to run. Tap the number to type one. Twenty to three hundred. The tempo is saved with the piece, so it travels to your other devices and into MusicXML. It is not an undoable edit. To put a written tempo mark on a bar — Allegro, ♩ = 120, a change partway through — use the tempo mark on the Form rail. This rail is the speed you audition at; that one is what the paper says." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-notes", title: "The Notes rail", section: "rails", order: 4,
    summary: "Voices, values, dot, tie, tuplet and accidentals — the rail you live on.",
    keywords: ["notes","palette","duration","value","whole","half","quarter","eighth","sixteenth","dot","tie","tuplet","triplet","accidental","sharp","flat","natural","voice","chord"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Voice","id":"voice"},{"level":2,"text":"The values","id":"the-values"},{"level":2,"text":"Dot","id":"dot"},{"level":2,"text":"Tie","id":"tie"},{"level":2,"text":"Tuplet","id":"tuplet"},{"level":2,"text":"Accidentals","id":"accidentals"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-notes.png\" alt=\"The Notes rail: the voice picker, the values, dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Notes rail: the voice picker, the values, dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"voice\">Voice</h2>\n<p><code>voice 1 ▾</code> at the left. Up to four voices on each staff, for an inner line under a melody or two-part counterpoint on one hand.</p>\n<p><strong>The voice follows the pen</strong>: tapping a note makes its voice the active one, and retyping, tying, deleting all act on that note's own voice. The picker mostly <em>shows</em> you where you are. <code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>1</code>–<code>4</code> switches.</p>\n<p>A voice that has nothing in a bar simply is not there — no stray whole-bar rest cluttering the staff. Voices 2 to 4 are tinted on screen so you can tell them apart; <strong>ink on paper is ink</strong>, and the export is monochrome. Stems and rests follow standard practice: voice 1 up and high, voice 2 down and low.</p>\n<p><code>⌘/Ctrl</code> <code>⇧</code> <code>↑</code> <code>↓</code> sends a note to the other staff without changing its voice — the left hand rising into the treble.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-values\">The values</h2>\n<p>Whole, half, quarter, eighth, sixteenth, and a <code>▾</code> for the double whole, 32nd and 64th. Tap to arm; tap with a selection to retype it. Tap the armed one again and the arming clears, which puts you in Select.</p>\n<p><code>1</code>–<code>7</code> on a keyboard, and the <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">chevrons</a> step the ladder.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dot\">Dot</h2>\n<p>Cycles none · dotted · double dotted. With a selection it dots everything selected, and undots when all of them are already dotted. A dot is a retype, so the all-or-nothing fit rule applies. <code>.</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"tie\">Tie</h2>\n<p>A tie joins two soundings of the <strong>same pitch</strong>. One note selected ties to the next note of that pitch in the staff — across a barline included. Two adjacent same-pitch notes selected ties those. A chord ties every pitch that has a match. All tied already, and it unties. <code>t</code></p>\n<p>Ties are re-derived after every edit, so one whose pitches no longer match simply goes.</p>\n<p>For <em>different</em> pitches you want a slur, on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-keys\">the Key · time · clef · marks rail</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tuplet\">Tuplet</h2>\n<p>Tap for a triplet; <strong>hold</strong> for a duplet, quintuplet, sextuplet or septuplet.</p>\n<p>With a selection it wraps that run — rests included — as <em>n in the time of m</em>. With nothing selected it arms: the first tap into plain rests opens a whole group, filled with tuplet rests, and each tap after that fills it. A tap inside an existing group takes that group's ratio whatever is armed. Delete every note of a group and it dissolves back into plain rests.</p>\n<h2 id=\"accidentals\">Accidentals</h2>\n<p>♯ ♭ ♮, with ♯♯ and ♭♭ behind the <code>▾</code>.</p>\n<p>With pitches selected they respell them. With nothing selected they arm <strong>one-shot</strong>: the next note you place carries the accidental and the button clears itself.</p>\n<p>Pressing the accidental a pitch already has takes it back to the key. Pressing one the key already implies makes it a <strong>cautionary</strong>, and again hides it. Chords stack their accidentals into columns automatically.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">∧ and ∨ chevrons</a> step a pitch through the whole ladder without touching the rail.</p>",
    text: "!The Notes rail: the voice picker, the values, dot, tie, tuplet, accidentals. Voice voice 1 ▾ at the left. Up to four voices on each staff, for an inner line under a melody or two-part counterpoint on one hand. The voice follows the pen: tapping a note makes its voice the active one, and retyping, tying, deleting all act on that note's own voice. The picker mostly shows you where you are. ⌘/Ctrl 1–4 switches. A voice that has nothing in a bar simply is not there — no stray whole-bar rest cluttering the staff. Voices 2 to 4 are tinted on screen so you can tell them apart; ink on paper is ink, and the export is monochrome. Stems and rests follow standard practice: voice 1 up and high, voice 2 down and low. ⌘/Ctrl ⇧ ↑ ↓ sends a note to the other staff without changing its voice — the left hand rising into the treble. The values Whole, half, quarter, eighth, sixteenth, and a ▾ for the double whole, 32nd and 64th. Tap to arm; tap with a selection to retype it. Tap the armed one again and the arming clears, which puts you in Select. 1–7 on a keyboard, and the chevrons step the ladder. Dot Cycles none · dotted · double dotted. With a selection it dots everything selected, and undots when all of them are already dotted. A dot is a retype, so the all-or-nothing fit rule applies. . Tie A tie joins two soundings of the same pitch. One note selected ties to the next note of that pitch in the staff — across a barline included. Two adjacent same-pitch notes selected ties those. A chord ties every pitch that has a match. All tied already, and it unties. t Ties are re-derived after every edit, so one whose pitches no longer match simply goes. For different pitches you want a slur, on the Key · time · clef · marks rail. Tuplet Tap for a triplet; hold for a duplet, quintuplet, sextuplet or septuplet. With a selection it wraps that run — rests included — as n in the time of m. With nothing selected it arms: the first tap into plain rests opens a whole group, filled with tuplet rests, and each tap after that fills it. A tap inside an existing group takes that group's ratio whatever is armed. Delete every note of a group and it dissolves back into plain rests. Accidentals ♯ ♭ ♮, with ♯♯ and ♭♭ behind the ▾. With pitches selected they respell them. With nothing selected they arm one-shot: the next note you place carries the accidental and the button clears itself. Pressing the accidental a pitch already has takes it back to the key. Pressing one the key already implies makes it a cautionary, and again hides it. Chords stack their accidentals into columns automatically. The ∧ and ∨ chevrons step a pitch through the whole ladder without touching the rail." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-keys", title: "Key · time · clef · marks", section: "rails", order: 5,
    summary: "Signatures and clefs anywhere in the piece, plus articulations, ornaments, slurs and rolled chords.",
    keywords: ["key","time","signature","clef","treble","bass","articulation","staccato","accent","tenuto","fermata","trill","mordent","turn","slur","glissando","arpeggio","rolled","ornament"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Key","id":"key"},{"level":2,"text":"Time","id":"time"},{"level":2,"text":"Clef","id":"clef"},{"level":2,"text":"Articulations and ornaments","id":"articulations-and-ornaments"},{"level":2,"text":"Slur","id":"slur"},{"level":2,"text":"Glissando","id":"glissando"},{"level":2,"text":"Rolled chords","id":"rolled-chords"},{"level":2,"text":"Pickup","id":"pickup"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-keys.png\" alt=\"The Key · time · clef · marks rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Key · time · clef · marks rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The first three buttons <strong>arm a cursor</strong>: tap the button, then tap where the change goes. The same change on the same place again removes it. <code>Esc</code> disarms.</p>\n<h2 id=\"key\">Key</h2>\n<p>Tap <strong>key</strong>, choose the signature from its <code>▾</code>, then tap the bar it starts on. It holds until the next change. Courtesy signatures at the change and cautionary accidentals after it follow standard practice, so you do not have to think about them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"time\">Time</h2>\n<p>Tap <strong>time</strong>, choose the signature, tap the bar. The bars from there are re-cut to the new metre by tick — notes move, sections stay with their bar numbers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clef\">Clef</h2>\n<p>Tap <strong>clef</strong>, choose it from the <code>▾</code>, then tap <strong>a staff and a beat</strong>. Unlike key and time, a clef change can land <em>inside</em> a bar, on any beat, because that is how a clef change is actually used.</p>\n<h2 id=\"articulations-and-ornaments\">Articulations and ornaments</h2>\n<p>Staccato, accent, tenuto, fermata, trill, mordent, the lower mordent, turn — the <strong>marks</strong> group. These follow the ordinary rule: with a selection they stamp it, and the same mark again takes it off. They are placed above or below the head as the stems require.</p>\n<p>Playback hears them — a fermata is held, a trill and a turn are played out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"slur\">Slur</h2>\n<p>A slur joins <em>different</em> pitches, where a tie joins the same one twice.</p>\n<p>Select a run and tap <strong>slur</strong>: it draws from the earliest selected note of a staff to the latest. Select one note and it slurs to the staff's next note, over rests. The same span again removes it. Slurs nest, may share a note as an end, are drawn on the head side, arch to clear what lies between, and split at a system break like a tie. Notes under a slur play legato.</p>\n<h2 id=\"glissando\">Glissando</h2>\n<p>Select two notes and tap <strong>gliss</strong>: a line runs from the first to the second.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rolled-chords\">Rolled chords</h2>\n<p><strong>Arp</strong> rolls the selected chord. Hold it for <em>rolled upward</em> and <em>rolled downward</em>, which add the arrow. Playback spreads the chord.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pickup\">Pickup</h2>\n<p>A partial opening or closing bar is on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-form\">the Form rail</a>, with the other bar operations.</p>",
    text: "!The Key · time · clef · marks rail. The first three buttons arm a cursor: tap the button, then tap where the change goes. The same change on the same place again removes it. Esc disarms. Key Tap key, choose the signature from its ▾, then tap the bar it starts on. It holds until the next change. Courtesy signatures at the change and cautionary accidentals after it follow standard practice, so you do not have to think about them. Time Tap time, choose the signature, tap the bar. The bars from there are re-cut to the new metre by tick — notes move, sections stay with their bar numbers. Clef Tap clef, choose it from the ▾, then tap a staff and a beat. Unlike key and time, a clef change can land inside a bar, on any beat, because that is how a clef change is actually used. Articulations and ornaments Staccato, accent, tenuto, fermata, trill, mordent, the lower mordent, turn — the marks group. These follow the ordinary rule: with a selection they stamp it, and the same mark again takes it off. They are placed above or below the head as the stems require. Playback hears them — a fermata is held, a trill and a turn are played out. Slur A slur joins different pitches, where a tie joins the same one twice. Select a run and tap slur: it draws from the earliest selected note of a staff to the latest. Select one note and it slurs to the staff's next note, over rests. The same span again removes it. Slurs nest, may share a note as an end, are drawn on the head side, arch to clear what lies between, and split at a system break like a tie. Notes under a slur play legato. Glissando Select two notes and tap gliss: a line runs from the first to the second. Rolled chords Arp rolls the selected chord. Hold it for rolled upward and rolled downward, which add the arrow. Playback spreads the chord. Pickup A partial opening or closing bar is on the Form rail, with the other bar operations." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-dynamics", title: "Dynamics · hairpins · text", section: "rails", order: 6,
    summary: "Everything that sits on a beat rather than on a note — and how to move it.",
    keywords: ["dynamics","dynamic","hairpin","crescendo","diminuendo","text","forte","piano","mezzo","sforzando","niente","slot","beat","expression"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"They live on beats, not on notes","id":"they-live-on-beats-not-on-notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Placing one","id":"placing-one"},{"level":2,"text":"Dynamics","id":"dynamics"},{"level":2,"text":"Hairpins","id":"hairpins"},{"level":2,"text":"Text","id":"text"},{"level":2,"text":"Selecting, moving, deleting","id":"selecting-moving-deleting"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-dynamics.png\" alt=\"The Dynamics · hairpins · text rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Dynamics · hairpins · text rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"they-live-on-beats-not-on-notes\">They live on beats, not on notes</h2>\n<p>A dynamic, a text or a hairpin belongs to a <strong>moment in the bar</strong>, not to a notehead. In 4/4 a bar has eight places one can sit: each beat and each <code>&amp;</code>. A slot need not hold a note at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-slot-grid\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-slot-grid\">The eight slots of a 4/4 bar.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"40\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"47\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"47\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"54\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"54\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"61\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"61\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"68\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"68\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"26\" y2=\"68\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"214\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"214\" y2=\"68\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-slot hp-fig-slot-strong\" x1=\"26\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"26\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"26\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot\" x1=\"49.5\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"49.5\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"49.5\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&amp;</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot hp-fig-slot-strong\" x1=\"73\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"73\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"73\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot\" x1=\"96.5\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"96.5\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"96.5\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&amp;</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot hp-fig-slot-strong\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"120\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot\" x1=\"143.5\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"143.5\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"143.5\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&amp;</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot hp-fig-slot-strong\" x1=\"167\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"167\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"167\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text><line class=\"hp-fig-slot\" x1=\"190.5\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"190.5\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"190.5\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\">&amp;</text><text class=\"hp-fig-accentink\" x=\"96.5\" y=\"102\" font-size=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\"></text><text class=\"hp-fig-label\" x=\"120\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">eight slots in a 4/4 bar — beats and their &amp;</text></svg><figcaption>The eight slots of a 4/4 bar.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>That is why deleting or retyping a note leaves your marks exactly where they were.</p>\n<h2 id=\"placing-one\">Placing one</h2>\n<p>Tap the mark on the rail; a ghost follows the pen and snaps to the nearest slot of the nearest staff; the tap places it. A <strong>hairpin takes three taps</strong>: the <code>&lt;</code> or <code>&gt;</code>, then the slot it starts on, then the slot it ends on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dynamics\">Dynamics</h2>\n<p><code>pp p mp mf f ff</code> on the rail. <strong>Hold</strong> any of them for the extremes — <code>pppp ppp fff ffff</code>. The <strong>sf</strong> button carries the sudden ones behind its own hold: <em>sf, sfz, sfp, fp, rfz</em>, which accent the notes on their slot rather than setting a level.</p>\n<p>Playback obeys them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hairpins\">Hairpins</h2>\n<p><code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code>. Hold for <strong>niente</strong> — a small circle at the closed tip, for a hairpin that grows from or fades to nothing.</p>\n<p>Two hairpins of the same kind on one staff never overlap: a new one owns its range and any it overlaps give way. A hairpin under a pedal line is fine — different kinds overlap freely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"text\">Text</h2>\n<p>Type the words in the box and tap <strong>set</strong>, then tap the slot. Common ones are chips behind the <code>▾</code> — <em>rit.</em>, <em>accel.</em>, <em>a tempo</em> and the rest — one tap instead of typing.</p>\n<p>A <strong>text line</strong> — <em>cresc. – – –</em>, with an optional word at its far end — is on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-piano\">the Piano rail</a> with the other spans.</p>\n<h2 id=\"selecting-moving-deleting\">Selecting, moving, deleting</h2>\n<p>Marks behave like notes. In Select mode a tap or a lasso takes one; <code>Delete</code> removes it; undo brings it back.</p>\n<p><strong>Drag one sideways</strong> and it slides slot by slot, across barlines and even across a system break. <code>←</code> and <code>→</code> nudge it a slot. <code>↑</code> and <code>↓</code> move it away from the staff, when the engraver's default sits somewhere you do not want.</p>\n<p>A selected hairpin shows <strong>two end handles</strong>: dragging the body moves both ends together, dragging a handle moves that end alone.</p>",
    text: "!The Dynamics · hairpins · text rail. They live on beats, not on notes A dynamic, a text or a hairpin belongs to a moment in the bar, not to a notehead. In 4/4 a bar has eight places one can sit: each beat and each &. A slot need not hold a note at all. !The eight slots of a 4/4 bar. That is why deleting or retyping a note leaves your marks exactly where they were. Placing one Tap the mark on the rail; a ghost follows the pen and snaps to the nearest slot of the nearest staff; the tap places it. A hairpin takes three taps: the < or , then the slot it starts on, then the slot it ends on. Dynamics pp p mp mf f ff on the rail. Hold any of them for the extremes — pppp ppp fff ffff. The sf button carries the sudden ones behind its own hold: sf, sfz, sfp, fp, rfz, which accent the notes on their slot rather than setting a level. Playback obeys them. Hairpins < and . Hold for niente — a small circle at the closed tip, for a hairpin that grows from or fades to nothing. Two hairpins of the same kind on one staff never overlap: a new one owns its range and any it overlaps give way. A hairpin under a pedal line is fine — different kinds overlap freely. Text Type the words in the box and tap set, then tap the slot. Common ones are chips behind the ▾ — rit., accel., a tempo and the rest — one tap instead of typing. A text line — cresc. – – –, with an optional word at its far end — is on the Piano rail with the other spans. Selecting, moving, deleting Marks behave like notes. In Select mode a tap or a lasso takes one; Delete removes it; undo brings it back. Drag one sideways and it slides slot by slot, across barlines and even across a system break. ← and → nudge it a slot. ↑ and ↓ move it away from the staff, when the engraver's default sits somewhere you do not want. A selected hairpin shows two end handles: dragging the body moves both ends together, dragging a handle moves that end alone." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-form", title: "The Form rail", section: "rails", order: 7,
    summary: "Barlines, repeats, endings, jumps, rehearsal marks, tempo marks, and the bars themselves.",
    keywords: ["form","barline","repeat","ending","volta","segno","coda","dc","ds","da capo","dal segno","fine","rehearsal","tempo mark","allegro","bar","measure","insert","delete","pickup","simile","bar repeat"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Barlines","id":"barlines"},{"level":2,"text":"Endings","id":"endings"},{"level":2,"text":"Signs and jumps","id":"signs-and-jumps"},{"level":2,"text":"Rehearsal marks","id":"rehearsal-marks"},{"level":2,"text":"Tempo marks","id":"tempo-marks"},{"level":2,"text":"Bar repeats","id":"bar-repeats"},{"level":2,"text":"Inserting and deleting bars","id":"inserting-and-deleting-bars"},{"level":2,"text":"Pickup","id":"pickup"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-form.png\" alt=\"The Form rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Form rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Form belongs to <strong>bars</strong>, not to beats. So every button here arms a cursor and the <strong>next tap on a bar</strong> acts; the same button on the same bar removes it; <code>Esc</code> cancels; each change is one undo step.</p>\n<h2 id=\"barlines\">Barlines</h2>\n<p><strong>barline</strong> sets the bar's closing line: double, final, or a repeat. Hold it for the repeat that <em>opens</em> a bar, and for a repeat count — <code>×2</code> up to <code>×9</code> — when a section should go round more than twice. The last bar of a piece always draws final whatever you do.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endings\">Endings</h2>\n<p><strong>ending</strong> puts a first-time bracket over a run of bars; hold it to choose the number, up to nine. Endings never overlap, and each number is unique inside its repeat.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signs-and-jumps\">Signs and jumps</h2>\n<p><strong>sign</strong> places a segno, a coda, a <em>To Coda</em> or a <em>Fine</em>. <strong>jump</strong> places the instruction: <em>D.C.</em>, <em>D.S.</em>, <em>D.C. al Fine</em>, <em>D.S. al Fine</em>, <em>D.C. al Coda</em>, <em>D.S. al Coda</em>. One jump to a bar.</p>\n<p>Playback unrolls all of it. The playhead still points at the bar in the score, so it jumps back at a repeat and forward at a <em>D.S.</em> — exactly what a player does.</p>\n<h2 id=\"rehearsal-marks\">Rehearsal marks</h2>\n<p><strong>rehearsal</strong> stamps a boxed mark on a bar. They letter themselves: the first is A, the next B, then C, and after Z comes AA — insert one in the middle and everything after it re-letters. Hold the button for a <strong>word</strong> instead (<em>Trio</em>) or for numbers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tempo-marks\">Tempo marks</h2>\n<p><strong>tempo-mark</strong> writes what the paper says: a metronome mark, a word like <em>Allegro</em>, or both. <strong>tempo-unit</strong> is the note the mark is counted in — hold it for an eighth or a dotted quarter instead of the plain quarter, for <code>♩. = 60</code> in a compound metre.</p>\n<p>Playback follows written tempo marks. The tempo on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-transport\">the Transport rail</a> is the speed you audition at; this is the speed the music <em>says</em>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"bar-repeats\">Bar repeats</h2>\n<p><strong>simile</strong> marks a bar as <code>%</code> — play the bar before again — or a two-bar repeat. Writing anything into the bar is the way out of it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"inserting-and-deleting-bars\">Inserting and deleting bars</h2>\n<p><strong>bar-insert</strong> puts an empty bar <strong>before</strong> the bar you tap. <strong>bar-delete</strong> takes the bar you tap, with everything it carried.</p>\n<p>Compose keeps the rest of the piece honest around them. A span ending in a deleted bar ends at the previous bar instead; endings and later spans shift along; a key, time or clef change the bar carried stays in force from the next bar; ties and slurs into the gap are cleaned. The last bar of a piece cannot be deleted.</p>\n<p>The editor always keeps one empty bar after the music to write into, and <strong>trims it away on export</strong>, so no stray measure reaches the PDF.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pickup\">Pickup</h2>\n<p><strong>pickup</strong> makes a partial bar — an anacrusis that opens the piece, or the short bar that completes one.</p>\n<p>It <strong>cuts the silence the bar already has</strong>. Tap it on an opening bar and the shared rest at the <em>front</em> goes; on a closing bar, the rest at the <em>back</em>. So write the pickup's notes first, then stamp it: in 12/8 a piece that starts on beat four becomes a one-beat bar, and the beaming, the rests and the tap grid all count from the barline that follows.</p>\n<p>Tap it again on a short bar to fill it back up. A bar that is empty, is in the middle of a phrase, or has a note where the silence would be cut is refused, unchanged.</p>\n<p>A pickup exports as an implicit bar 0, the way Dorico, MuseScore and Finale write one, and imports the same way.</p>",
    text: "!The Form rail. Form belongs to bars, not to beats. So every button here arms a cursor and the next tap on a bar acts; the same button on the same bar removes it; Esc cancels; each change is one undo step. Barlines barline sets the bar's closing line: double, final, or a repeat. Hold it for the repeat that opens a bar, and for a repeat count — ×2 up to ×9 — when a section should go round more than twice. The last bar of a piece always draws final whatever you do. Endings ending puts a first-time bracket over a run of bars; hold it to choose the number, up to nine. Endings never overlap, and each number is unique inside its repeat. Signs and jumps sign places a segno, a coda, a To Coda or a Fine. jump places the instruction: D.C., D.S., D.C. al Fine, D.S. al Fine, D.C. al Coda, D.S. al Coda. One jump to a bar. Playback unrolls all of it. The playhead still points at the bar in the score, so it jumps back at a repeat and forward at a D.S. — exactly what a player does. Rehearsal marks rehearsal stamps a boxed mark on a bar. They letter themselves: the first is A, the next B, then C, and after Z comes AA — insert one in the middle and everything after it re-letters. Hold the button for a word instead (Trio) or for numbers. Tempo marks tempo-mark writes what the paper says: a metronome mark, a word like Allegro, or both. tempo-unit is the note the mark is counted in — hold it for an eighth or a dotted quarter instead of the plain quarter, for ♩. = 60 in a compound metre. Playback follows written tempo marks. The tempo on the Transport rail is the speed you audition at; this is the speed the music says. Bar repeats simile marks a bar as % — play the bar before again — or a two-bar repeat. Writing anything into the bar is the way out of it. Inserting and deleting bars bar-insert puts an empty bar before the bar you tap. bar-delete takes the bar you tap, with everything it carried. Compose keeps the rest of the piece honest around them. A span ending in a deleted bar ends at the previous bar instead; endings and later spans shift along; a key, time or clef change the bar carried stays in force from the next bar; ties and slurs into the gap are cleaned. The last bar of a piece cannot be deleted. The editor always keeps one empty bar after the music to write into, and trims it away on export, so no stray measure reaches the PDF. Pickup pickup makes a partial bar — an anacrusis that opens the piece, or the short bar that completes one. It cuts the silence the bar already has. Tap it on an opening bar and the shared rest at the front goes; on a closing bar, the rest at the back. So write the pickup's notes first, then stamp it: in 12/8 a piece that starts on beat four becomes a one-beat bar, and the beaming, the rests and the tap grid all count from the barline that follows. Tap it again on a short bar to fill it back up. A bar that is empty, is in the middle of a phrase, or has a note where the silence would be cut is refused, unchanged. A pickup exports as an implicit bar 0, the way Dorico, MuseScore and Finale write one, and imports the same way." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-piano", title: "The Piano rail", section: "rails", order: 8,
    summary: "Pedal, octave lines, hand marks and fingering.",
    keywords: ["piano","pedal","sustain","sostenuto","ottava","8va","8vb","15ma","octave","fingering","finger","hand","mano","text line","cresc"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Pedal","id":"pedal"},{"level":2,"text":"Octave lines","id":"octave-lines"},{"level":2,"text":"Text lines","id":"text-lines"},{"level":2,"text":"Hand marks","id":"hand-marks"},{"level":2,"text":"Fingering","id":"fingering"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-piano.png\" alt=\"The Piano rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Piano rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"pedal\">Pedal</h2>\n<p><strong>pedal</strong> is a span on the same half-beat grid the <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-dynamics\">dynamics</a> use: tap the button, tap the slot it starts on, tap the slot it lifts. It selects, drags, stretches by an end handle and deletes exactly like a hairpin.</p>\n<p>Hold the button for the styles: the plain <strong>Ped. ✱</strong> sign with no line, or <strong>Sost. Ped.</strong> for the middle pedal.</p>\n<p>A pedal that starts where the last one ended is drawn as a <strong>retake</strong> — Compose reads that off the music, there is no flag to set.</p>\n<p><strong>It is heard.</strong> Every note under a pedal span sounds until the pedal lifts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"octave-lines\">Octave lines</h2>\n<p><strong>ottava</strong> draws <em>8va</em> above or <em>8vb</em> below; hold for <strong>15ma</strong> and <em>15mb</em>.</p>\n<p>An octave line <strong>changes the drawing, never the pitch</strong>. The model keeps what sounds, and the engraver draws the heads an octave out of the way. Playback, MusicXML, transposing by drag and the accidental memory all see the real pitch, and removing the line puts the heads back where they sound. That is the opposite of transposing the notes by hand, which is what makes it safe.</p>\n<h2 id=\"text-lines\">Text lines</h2>\n<p><strong>textline</strong> is a dashed span with a word at its start and, if you want, another at its end: <em>cresc. – – –</em>, <em>una corda …… tre corde</em>. Two taps, like a hairpin.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hand-marks\">Hand marks</h2>\n<p><strong>hands</strong> stamps <em>m.d.</em> and <em>m.s.</em> — which hand takes the passage. They are text, placed on a slot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fingering\">Fingering</h2>\n<p><strong>finger</strong> arms a digit, 1 to 5. Then <strong>each tap on a notehead stamps it</strong>, and the digit stays armed, so a whole passage is fingered without going back to the rail between notes. A chord carries one digit per notehead, because fingering belongs to the pitch, like a tie or an accidental.</p>\n<p>Tap the same digit on a head again to take it off. <code>Esc</code>, or the digit on the rail, disarms.</p>",
    text: "!The Piano rail. Pedal pedal is a span on the same half-beat grid the dynamics use: tap the button, tap the slot it starts on, tap the slot it lifts. It selects, drags, stretches by an end handle and deletes exactly like a hairpin. Hold the button for the styles: the plain Ped. ✱ sign with no line, or Sost. Ped. for the middle pedal. A pedal that starts where the last one ended is drawn as a retake — Compose reads that off the music, there is no flag to set. It is heard. Every note under a pedal span sounds until the pedal lifts. Octave lines ottava draws 8va above or 8vb below; hold for 15ma and 15mb. An octave line changes the drawing, never the pitch. The model keeps what sounds, and the engraver draws the heads an octave out of the way. Playback, MusicXML, transposing by drag and the accidental memory all see the real pitch, and removing the line puts the heads back where they sound. That is the opposite of transposing the notes by hand, which is what makes it safe. Text lines textline is a dashed span with a word at its start and, if you want, another at its end: cresc. – – –, una corda …… tre corde. Two taps, like a hairpin. Hand marks hands stamps m.d. and m.s. — which hand takes the passage. They are text, placed on a slot. Fingering finger arms a digit, 1 to 5. Then each tap on a notehead stamps it, and the digit stays armed, so a whole passage is fingered without going back to the rail between notes. A chord carries one digit per notehead, because fingering belongs to the pitch, like a tie or an accidental. Tap the same digit on a head again to take it off. Esc, or the digit on the rail, disarms." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "rail-marks", title: "Grace · tremolo · marks", section: "rails", order: 9,
    summary: "Grace notes, tremolo, trill lines, the rarer articulations, and two engraving overrides.",
    keywords: ["grace","acciaccatura","appoggiatura","tremolo","trill","portato","breath","caesura","turn","stem","beam","marks","ornament","slash"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Grace notes","id":"grace-notes"},{"level":2,"text":"Tremolo","id":"tremolo"},{"level":2,"text":"Trills and the rarer marks","id":"trills-and-the-rarer-marks"},{"level":2,"text":"Two engraving overrides","id":"two-engraving-overrides"}],
    html: "<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/rail-marks.png\" alt=\"The Grace · tremolo · marks rail.\" width=\"2360\" height=\"108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Grace · tremolo · marks rail.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The second Notes lane, for what would not fit on the first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"grace-notes\">Grace notes</h2>\n<p><strong>grace</strong> is a modifier on the armed value, like the dot: turn it on, and the value armed on <a href=\"#/compose/help/rail-notes\">the Notes rail</a> becomes the <em>grace's</em> value — an eighth, a sixteenth or a 32nd. Tap the staff and a grace lands <strong>before the note it precedes</strong> instead of taking time of its own.</p>\n<p>Hold the button for the <strong>slash</strong> — the acciaccatura — and for clearing every grace off the selection.</p>\n<p><strong>grace-chord</strong> adds another pitch to the grace you just placed, so a grace can be a chord.</p>\n<p>A grace note takes no time from the bar, so it never disturbs the sum. It is stored on the note it belongs to, which is also how MusicXML says it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tremolo\">Tremolo</h2>\n<p><strong>trem</strong> stamps one, two or three beams on the selected notes. The same count again clears it. Playback plays it out.</p>\n<h2 id=\"trills-and-the-rarer-marks\">Trills and the rarer marks</h2>\n<p><strong>art</strong> here carries what the first marks group did not have room for: the <strong>trill line</strong> — the wavy extension after a <code>tr</code>, with an accidental over it when the trill is not diatonic — and <strong>portato</strong>, <strong>breath</strong>, <strong>caesura</strong>, the <strong>inverted turn</strong> and the <strong>delayed turn</strong>.</p>\n<p>They work like every other mark: with a selection they stamp it, the same one again takes it off, and playback hears them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"two-engraving-overrides\">Two engraving overrides</h2>\n<p>Compose decides stems and beam groups by standard practice, and is right almost always. For the times it is not:</p>\n<p><strong>stem</strong> forces a note's stem up or down. <strong>beam</strong> breaks the beam group at the note, so a new group starts there.</p>\n<p>Both are overrides of a good default, not settings to manage — use them where the music means something the rules cannot know, and leave them alone everywhere else. They travel into the PDF and into MusicXML.</p>",
    text: "!The Grace · tremolo · marks rail. The second Notes lane, for what would not fit on the first. Grace notes grace is a modifier on the armed value, like the dot: turn it on, and the value armed on the Notes rail becomes the grace's value — an eighth, a sixteenth or a 32nd. Tap the staff and a grace lands before the note it precedes instead of taking time of its own. Hold the button for the slash — the acciaccatura — and for clearing every grace off the selection. grace-chord adds another pitch to the grace you just placed, so a grace can be a chord. A grace note takes no time from the bar, so it never disturbs the sum. It is stored on the note it belongs to, which is also how MusicXML says it. Tremolo trem stamps one, two or three beams on the selected notes. The same count again clears it. Playback plays it out. Trills and the rarer marks art here carries what the first marks group did not have room for: the trill line — the wavy extension after a tr, with an accidental over it when the trill is not diatonic — and portato, breath, caesura, the inverted turn and the delayed turn. They work like every other mark: with a selection they stamp it, the same one again takes it off, and playback hears them. Two engraving overrides Compose decides stems and beam groups by standard practice, and is right almost always. For the times it is not: stem forces a note's stem up or down. beam breaks the beam group at the note, so a new group starts there. Both are overrides of a good default, not settings to manage — use them where the music means something the rules cannot know, and leave them alone everywhere else. They travel into the PDF and into MusicXML." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "saving", title: "Saving and your library", section: "keeping", order: 1,
    summary: "There is no save button — and what an account adds when you want a backup.",
    keywords: ["save","saving","library","list","sync","account","backup","title","composer","tags","search","delete","export data"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"There is no save button","id":"there-is-no-save-button"},{"level":2,"text":"The library","id":"the-library"},{"level":2,"text":"With an account","id":"with-an-account"},{"level":2,"text":"What stays on the device on purpose","id":"what-stays-on-the-device-on-purpose"},{"level":2,"text":"Linking a piece to practice","id":"linking-a-piece-to-practice"}],
    html: "<h2 id=\"there-is-no-save-button\">There is no save button</h2>\n<p>Every committed edit is written to this device at once — a placement, a delete, a retype, the drop of a drag. Close the tab mid-bar and it is already there. Reopening restores the piece and the zoom you left it at.</p>\n<p>That is the whole of it without an account: your compositions live <strong>in your browser, on your device</strong>, and never leave it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-library\">The library</h2>\n<p><code>‹</code> from the editor is the compositions list.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/compositions-list.png\" alt=\"The compositions list.\" width=\"1112\" height=\"422\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The compositions list.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul><li><strong>new</strong> starts one; <strong>import</strong> reads MusicXML files, several at a time — see <a href=\"#/compose/help/musicxml\">MusicXML in and out</a>.</li><li><strong>Search</strong> by title, composer or tag. Sort by recent, title or composer, and group by composer.</li><li><strong>Tap</strong> a row to open it. <strong>Hold</strong> a row for its details — title, composer, tags — and to delete it.</li></ul>\n<p>The details sheet is the same one that greets a new composition. The title also sits in the editor's header: tap it there.</p>\n<h2 id=\"with-an-account\">With an account</h2>\n<p>Sign in with an emailed code — there is no password — and your compositions sync like everything else in the logbook: your practice, your goals, your scores. Write on the iPad, open the laptop, carry on.</p>\n<p>A composition is capped at a megabyte of music, which is about a hundred bars of solid sixteenths on both staves. The account sheet's <strong>download everything</strong> includes your compositions, and deleting the account wipes them with the rest.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-stays-on-the-device-on-purpose\">What stays on the device on purpose</h2>\n<p>Your zoom, which rails you show, <a href=\"#/compose/help/input\">Pen ∣ Touch</a>, <a href=\"#/compose/help/gestures\">Gesture</a> and your <a href=\"#/compose/help/favorites\">Favorites</a> are remembered <strong>per device</strong> and are not synced. They are habits of the thing in your hands — the iPad wants different rails from the laptop — not properties of the music.</p>\n<h2 id=\"linking-a-piece-to-practice\">Linking a piece to practice</h2>\n<p>A composition can be linked to a goal, exactly as a score can, and <em>practice this</em> starts the clock on it. Your own piece becomes a thing you log hours against.</p>",
    text: "There is no save button Every committed edit is written to this device at once — a placement, a delete, a retype, the drop of a drag. Close the tab mid-bar and it is already there. Reopening restores the piece and the zoom you left it at. That is the whole of it without an account: your compositions live in your browser, on your device, and never leave it. The library ‹ from the editor is the compositions list. !The compositions list. - new starts one; import reads MusicXML files, several at a time — see MusicXML in and out. - Search by title, composer or tag. Sort by recent, title or composer, and group by composer. - Tap a row to open it. Hold a row for its details — title, composer, tags — and to delete it. The details sheet is the same one that greets a new composition. The title also sits in the editor's header: tap it there. With an account Sign in with an emailed code — there is no password — and your compositions sync like everything else in the logbook: your practice, your goals, your scores. Write on the iPad, open the laptop, carry on. A composition is capped at a megabyte of music, which is about a hundred bars of solid sixteenths on both staves. The account sheet's download everything includes your compositions, and deleting the account wipes them with the rest. What stays on the device on purpose Your zoom, which rails you show, Pen ∣ Touch, Gesture and your Favorites are remembered per device and are not synced. They are habits of the thing in your hands — the iPad wants different rails from the laptop — not properties of the music. Linking a piece to practice A composition can be linked to a goal, exactly as a score can, and practice this starts the clock on it. Your own piece becomes a thing you log hours against." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "export-pdf", title: "Export a PDF · Add to Scores", section: "keeping", order: 2,
    summary: "Real vector pages — sized, paged and previewed — saved, shared, or dropped into your library.",
    keywords: ["export","pdf","print","page","letter","a4","margins","staff size","preview","share","download","scores","add to scores"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"It is a real PDF","id":"it-is-a-real-pdf"},{"level":2,"text":"The controls","id":"the-controls"},{"level":2,"text":"The preview is the page","id":"the-preview-is-the-page"},{"level":2,"text":"Getting the file","id":"getting-the-file"},{"level":2,"text":"Add to Scores","id":"add-to-scores"}],
    html: "<p><strong>File ▾ → Export PDF</strong> opens the export sheet. <strong>File ▾ → Save to Scores as PDF</strong> does the same thing with the library as its target.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/export-sheet.png\" alt=\"The export sheet, with a live preview of page 1.\" width=\"2388\" height=\"1668\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The export sheet, with a live preview of page 1.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"it-is-a-real-pdf\">It is a real PDF</h2>\n<p>Vector outlines, not a picture of a page. The notes are drawn from Bravura's own outlines and the words are embedded font subsets, so it prints at any size and a reader can select the title. There is no raster fallback anywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-controls\">The controls</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Layout</strong> — opens <a href=\"#/compose/help/layout\">the Layout editor</a>, where you arrange bars into rows on the paper itself. The hint beside it reads <em>automatic</em>, or how many pins you have set.</li><li><strong>size</strong> — the staff size, from 1.4 to 2.5 mm a staff space, in small steps. The readout shows the staff height and the page count as you go.</li><li><strong>page</strong> — Letter or A4.</li><li><strong>margins</strong> — narrow, normal or wide.</li><li><strong>header</strong> — the title and composer on page 1, and a running head with the page number after it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-preview-is-the-page\">The preview is the page</h2>\n<p>The preview is the <strong>same engraver</strong> drawing the <strong>same plan</strong> the PDF is built from — not an approximation. The systems on preview page 2 are the systems on PDF page 2, and the preview does not clip at the margin where the PDF would not, so a tempo word above the first staff or a pedal line under the last is visible rather than quietly hidden.</p>\n<p>When the piece runs past one page a <strong>pager</strong> appears under the paper — <em>‹ page 1 of 3 ›</em> — with the running head as the PDF sets it.</p>\n<p>A system is never split across a page, page 1 keeps room for the title block, and every page keeps enough air that nothing prints into a margin. Trailing empty bars are trimmed first; an empty piece prints its eight bars of manuscript.</p>\n<h2 id=\"getting-the-file\">Getting the file</h2>\n<p><strong>Save PDF</strong> offers <strong>Save to device</strong> — straight into Downloads or Files — or <strong>Share…</strong>, where a share sheet exists. Both are there because the share sheet alone does not save.</p>\n<h2 id=\"add-to-scores\">Add to Scores</h2>\n<p><strong>Add to Scores</strong> puts the PDF into your <a href=\"#/scores\">Scores</a> library in one tap, with the title, composer and tags it already has, a thumbnail and rendered pages like any other upload.</p>\n<p>The composition <strong>remembers the score it made</strong>. Send it again after changing the music and it <strong>replaces that score's file in place</strong>: the pages re-render, the thumbnail is remade, and your bookmarks, your Pencil ink and the goal it is linked to all stay. You can annotate your own engraving and keep revising the engraving.</p>\n<p>Send unchanged bytes and nothing is duplicated — the same score reopens.</p>",
    text: "File ▾ → Export PDF opens the export sheet. File ▾ → Save to Scores as PDF does the same thing with the library as its target. !The export sheet, with a live preview of page 1. It is a real PDF Vector outlines, not a picture of a page. The notes are drawn from Bravura's own outlines and the words are embedded font subsets, so it prints at any size and a reader can select the title. There is no raster fallback anywhere. The controls - Layout — opens the Layout editor, where you arrange bars into rows on the paper itself. The hint beside it reads automatic, or how many pins you have set. - size — the staff size, from 1.4 to 2.5 mm a staff space, in small steps. The readout shows the staff height and the page count as you go. - page — Letter or A4. - margins — narrow, normal or wide. - header — the title and composer on page 1, and a running head with the page number after it. The preview is the page The preview is the same engraver drawing the same plan the PDF is built from — not an approximation. The systems on preview page 2 are the systems on PDF page 2, and the preview does not clip at the margin where the PDF would not, so a tempo word above the first staff or a pedal line under the last is visible rather than quietly hidden. When the piece runs past one page a pager appears under the paper — ‹ page 1 of 3 › — with the running head as the PDF sets it. A system is never split across a page, page 1 keeps room for the title block, and every page keeps enough air that nothing prints into a margin. Trailing empty bars are trimmed first; an empty piece prints its eight bars of manuscript. Getting the file Save PDF offers Save to device — straight into Downloads or Files — or Share…, where a share sheet exists. Both are there because the share sheet alone does not save. Add to Scores Add to Scores puts the PDF into your Scores library in one tap, with the title, composer and tags it already has, a thumbnail and rendered pages like any other upload. The composition remembers the score it made. Send it again after changing the music and it replaces that score's file in place: the pages re-render, the thumbnail is remade, and your bookmarks, your Pencil ink and the goal it is linked to all stay. You can annotate your own engraving and keep revising the engraving. Send unchanged bytes and nothing is duplicated — the same score reopens." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "layout", title: "The Layout editor", section: "keeping", order: 3,
    summary: "Say which bars share a row and how wide each one is — on the paper, not on the screen.",
    keywords: ["layout","pin","pins","break","keep","row","system","width","weight","lock","reset","paper","page","justify"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Pins, not a second layout","id":"pins-not-a-second-layout"},{"level":2,"text":"What you can pin","id":"what-you-can-pin"},{"level":2,"text":"Working in it","id":"working-in-it"},{"level":2,"text":"The marks","id":"the-marks"}],
    html: "<p>The engraver decides the layout, and is right most of the time. When it is not — bar 10 is dense and wants more room, or you want bars 1–4 on the first row because that is the phrase — the Layout editor lets you say so.</p>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure hp-shot\"><img src=\"/img/help/layout-view.png\" alt=\"The Layout editor: the plan's own pages, with a handle on every barline.\" width=\"2388\" height=\"1668\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"><figcaption>The Layout editor: the plan's own pages, with a handle on every barline.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Open it from the <strong>Layout</strong> row at the top of <a href=\"#/compose/help/export-pdf\">the export sheet</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pins-not-a-second-layout\">Pins, not a second layout</h2>\n<p>A pin is <strong>one override</strong> of an automatic decision. A piece with no pins lays out exactly as it always did. Pin three barlines and the other sixty bars still flow by themselves.</p>\n<p>Pins are <strong>paper only</strong>. The editor wraps to the width of your screen, where <em>&quot;bars 1–4 on row 1&quot;</em> has no honest meaning, so the editor never reads them — only the export does. And they <strong>ride their bar</strong>: insert or delete a bar elsewhere and they do not shift, they sync with the piece, and an older copy of the app simply ignores them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-you-can-pin\">What you can pin</h2>\n<figure class=\"hp-figure\"><svg class=\"hp-fig\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 120\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"fg-layout-pins\" preserveAspectRatio=\"xMidYMid meet\"><title id=\"fg-layout-pins\">A break, a keep, and a bar given more width.</title><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"46\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"53\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"53\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"60\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"67\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"67\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-staff\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"74\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"18\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"18\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"78\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"78\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"120\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"176\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"176\" y2=\"74\"/><line class=\"hp-fig-ink\" x1=\"222\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"74\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"48\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"99\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2</text><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"148\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\">3</text><text class=\"hp-fig-small\" x=\"199\" y=\"36\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4</text><polyline class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" points=\"78,26 78,38\"/><polygon class=\"hp-fig-arrow\" points=\"78,38 75.6,33 80.4,33\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-accentword\" x=\"78\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\">break</text><path class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" d=\"M126 36 Q148 22 170 36\" fill=\"none\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-accentword\" x=\"148\" y=\"20\" text-anchor=\"middle\">keep</text><line class=\"hp-fig-stroke\" x1=\"176\" y1=\"84\" x2=\"222\" y2=\"84\"/><text class=\"hp-fig-accentword\" x=\"199\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\">128 %</text></svg><figcaption>A break, a keep, and a bar given more width.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul><li><strong>Break here</strong> — the row ends at this barline, always.</li><li><strong>Keep together</strong> — the row may <em>not</em> end here; this bar and the next share a row.</li><li><strong>A width</strong> — how much of its row a bar takes, relative to the others.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"working-in-it\">Working in it</h2>\n<p>The pages are the export's own pages, stacked and scrolling, drawn by the same painter. <code>−</code> and <code>+</code> zoom them; a phone needs it.</p>\n<p><strong>Every barline has a handle</strong> — invisible, at least a fingertip wide, the height of the grand staff. A barline is a hair thick; its target is not.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Tap</strong> a handle for its menu: <em>Break here</em>, <em>Keep together</em>, and <em>Reset this bar's width</em> when it has one. Whichever is already set reads as <em>Remove the break</em> or <em>Remove the keep</em>.</li><li><strong>Drag</strong> a handle sideways and the bar to its left takes the width under your pointer; the rest of the row gives or takes in proportion and stays justified. One undo step per drag. The drag stops rather than squeezing a bar past what is readable.</li><li><strong>The chip in the left margin</strong> of each row locks the row: <em>Lock this row</em> pins exactly these bars together whatever happens around them, and <em>Release this row</em> takes every pin off them again.</li></ul>\n<p>A <strong>finger, a Pencil and a mouse all do the same thing</strong> here. There is no Pen ∣ Touch switch in this view, and a finger that starts on a handle can still scroll the pages.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-marks\">The marks</h2>\n<p>A break shows a small ⏎ flag above the barline, a keep shows a link arc over it, and a weighted bar gets a rule under it with its percentage. They are drawn beside the page, never in it — <strong>they cannot print</strong>.</p>\n<p>If a row will not fit at the widths you have asked for, the row is marked as tight. Nothing is silently squeezed.</p>\n<p><strong>Reset</strong> in the bar takes every pin off the piece and returns it to automatic.</p>",
    text: "The engraver decides the layout, and is right most of the time. When it is not — bar 10 is dense and wants more room, or you want bars 1–4 on the first row because that is the phrase — the Layout editor lets you say so. !The Layout editor: the plan's own pages, with a handle on every barline. Open it from the Layout row at the top of the export sheet. Pins, not a second layout A pin is one override of an automatic decision. A piece with no pins lays out exactly as it always did. Pin three barlines and the other sixty bars still flow by themselves. Pins are paper only. The editor wraps to the width of your screen, where \"bars 1–4 on row 1\" has no honest meaning, so the editor never reads them — only the export does. And they ride their bar: insert or delete a bar elsewhere and they do not shift, they sync with the piece, and an older copy of the app simply ignores them. What you can pin !A break, a keep, and a bar given more width. - Break here — the row ends at this barline, always. - Keep together — the row may not end here; this bar and the next share a row. - A width — how much of its row a bar takes, relative to the others. Working in it The pages are the export's own pages, stacked and scrolling, drawn by the same painter. − and + zoom them; a phone needs it. Every barline has a handle — invisible, at least a fingertip wide, the height of the grand staff. A barline is a hair thick; its target is not. - Tap a handle for its menu: Break here, Keep together, and Reset this bar's width when it has one. Whichever is already set reads as Remove the break or Remove the keep. - Drag a handle sideways and the bar to its left takes the width under your pointer; the rest of the row gives or takes in proportion and stays justified. One undo step per drag. The drag stops rather than squeezing a bar past what is readable. - The chip in the left margin of each row locks the row: Lock this row pins exactly these bars together whatever happens around them, and Release this row takes every pin off them again. A finger, a Pencil and a mouse all do the same thing here. There is no Pen ∣ Touch switch in this view, and a finger that starts on a handle can still scroll the pages. The marks A break shows a small ⏎ flag above the barline, a keep shows a link arc over it, and a weighted bar gets a rule under it with its percentage. They are drawn beside the page, never in it — they cannot print. If a row will not fit at the widths you have asked for, the row is marked as tight. Nothing is silently squeezed. Reset in the bar takes every pin off the piece and returns it to automatic." },
  { tool: "compose", slug: "musicxml", title: "MusicXML in and out", section: "keeping", order: 4,
    summary: "Take a piece to Sibelius, MuseScore, Finale or Dorico — and bring one back.",
    keywords: ["musicxml","xml","mxl","import","export","sibelius","musescore","finale","dorico","interchange","open","file"],
    headings: [{"level":2,"text":"Out","id":"out"},{"level":2,"text":"In","id":"in"},{"level":2,"text":"What does not travel","id":"what-does-not-travel"},{"level":2,"text":"MIDI","id":"midi"}],
    html: "<h2 id=\"out\">Out</h2>\n<p><strong>File ▾ → Export MusicXML</strong> writes a MusicXML 4.0 part-wise file and offers the same save-or-share choice the PDF does.</p>\n<p>It is a full serialisation, not a sketch: both staves, all four voices, chords, ties, tuplets, cross-staff notes, articulations, ornaments, glissandi, rolled chords, grace notes, tremolo, trills, stem and beam overrides, dynamics, hairpins, text and text lines, pedal and octave lines at their exact tick, barlines, repeats, endings, jumps, rehearsal marks, tempo marks, bar repeats, rest offsets, fingering, and the accidentals spelled the way the engraver drew them.</p>\n<p>Pitch is never a MIDI number anywhere in Compose, so <strong>spelling survives the round trip</strong>: a C♯♯ arrives as a C♯♯, not as a D.</p>\n<p>A pickup goes out as an implicit bar 0 with the bars after it counting from 1 — how Dorico, MuseScore and Finale write one.</p>\n<h2 id=\"in\">In</h2>\n<p><strong>import</strong> on the compositions list takes <code>.musicxml</code>, <code>.xml</code> and compressed <code>.mxl</code> files, several at once. Each becomes a new composition tagged <em>imported</em>, and the first one opens.</p>\n<p>Compose reads part-wise and time-wise scores from any program. What it cannot represent it says so about, naming the bar, rather than quietly dropping it — and what it does drop is listed in the console for the file.</p>\n<p>A bar every voice leaves short is kept as a short bar: the first bar, or one the file marks implicit, counted from the end as a pickup; any other from its start.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-does-not-travel\">What does not travel</h2>\n<p>Layout pins (see <a href=\"#/compose/help/layout\">the Layout editor</a>) are not written or read — a page layout is a property of <em>your</em> paper, not of the music. Lyrics, chord symbols and parts are not in Compose yet, so a file carrying them arrives without them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"midi\">MIDI</h2>\n<p>Not yet. MusicXML carries more and every one of those programs reads it.</p>",
    text: "Out File ▾ → Export MusicXML writes a MusicXML 4.0 part-wise file and offers the same save-or-share choice the PDF does. It is a full serialisation, not a sketch: both staves, all four voices, chords, ties, tuplets, cross-staff notes, articulations, ornaments, glissandi, rolled chords, grace notes, tremolo, trills, stem and beam overrides, dynamics, hairpins, text and text lines, pedal and octave lines at their exact tick, barlines, repeats, endings, jumps, rehearsal marks, tempo marks, bar repeats, rest offsets, fingering, and the accidentals spelled the way the engraver drew them. Pitch is never a MIDI number anywhere in Compose, so spelling survives the round trip: a C♯♯ arrives as a C♯♯, not as a D. A pickup goes out as an implicit bar 0 with the bars after it counting from 1 — how Dorico, MuseScore and Finale write one. In import on the compositions list takes .musicxml, .xml and compressed .mxl files, several at once. Each becomes a new composition tagged imported, and the first one opens. Compose reads part-wise and time-wise scores from any program. What it cannot represent it says so about, naming the bar, rather than quietly dropping it — and what it does drop is listed in the console for the file. A bar every voice leaves short is kept as a short bar: the first bar, or one the file marks implicit, counted from the end as a pickup; any other from its start. What does not travel Layout pins (see the Layout editor) are not written or read — a page layout is a property of your paper, not of the music. Lyrics, chord symbols and parts are not in Compose yet, so a file carrying them arrives without them. MIDI Not yet. MusicXML carries more and every one of those programs reads it." },
];

export const BY_SLUG = Object.fromEntries(ARTICLES.map((a) => [a.slug, a]));

/** The articles of one tool, in reading order. */
export const forTool = (tool) => ARTICLES.filter((a) => a.tool === tool);
/** The sections of one tool that actually have articles, in order. */
export const sectionsFor = (tool) => SECTIONS.filter((s) => s.tool === tool && ARTICLES.some((a) => a.tool === tool && a.section === s.id));
